/* Section data for pcap-investigation.js (English and Korean). Example outputs are illustrative placeholders, not engine runs. */
window.DCI_PCAP_DATA = {"sections": [{"id": "setup-and-workflow", "title": {"en": "Setup and the standard workflow", "ko": "준비와 표준 분석 순서"}, "html": {"en": "<div class=\"example-label\" markdown=\"0\"><strong>Scope of this section.</strong> <ul>\n<li>One fixed order of work that answers most investigation questions</li>\n<li>Step 0: file overview with <code>capinfos</code></li>\n<li>Turning the PCAP into Zeek logs and Snort 2 alerts, and reading them with <code>zeek-cut</code> and <code>grep</code></li>\n<li>The four Wireshark techniques that replace almost every tshark option</li>\n<li>Why every answer is in UTC, and how to build a timeline</li>\n</ul></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"pcap-workflow\">The standard workflow</h3>\n<p>Most PCAP investigations tell the same story: one Windows (or other) machine received something it should not have, and you have to prove who, what, when, from where and what happened next. Doing the steps in the same order every time is faster than reading the questions first.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>0 · capinfos — time range, size</li>\n<li>1 · Zeek + Snort — logs and alerts</li>\n<li>2 · Suspect IP — alerts, exe/doc receiver</li>\n<li>3 · Identify the host — MAC, hostname, user, OS</li>\n<li>4 · Downloads — URLs, sizes, extract, hash</li>\n<li>5 · Classify — alert names, hash lookup</li>\n<li>6 · After the download — C2, spread to DC</li>\n<li>7 · Timeline in UTC</li>\n<li>8 · Short written answer</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Read top to bottom. Steps 2–3 answer \"who\", step 4 \"what and from where\", steps 5–6 \"what it is and what it did\", and step 7 puts it all in order for the final written explanation.</p>\n<h3 id=\"pcap-capinfos\">Step 0: file overview</h3>\n<pre><code>capinfos case.pcap\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><code>capinfos</code> ships with Wireshark and summarises a capture: number of packets, first and last packet time, size.</li>\n</ul>\n<pre><code>Number of packets:   xx k\nFirst packet time:   ...\nLast packet time:    ...\n</code></pre>\n<p>Wireshark: <strong>Statistics → Capture File Properties</strong> (same information) and <strong>Statistics → Protocol Hierarchy</strong> (how much HTTP, TLS, Kerberos, DHCP, SMB there is).</p>\n<h3 id=\"pcap-zeek\">Generating Zeek logs</h3>\n<pre><code>mkdir zeek_out &amp;&amp; cd zeek_out\nzeek -C -r ../case.pcap local\nls *.log\ncd ..\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><code>-r ../case.pcap</code>: read this file instead of a live interface.</li>\n<li><code>-C</code>: ignore checksum errors. Captures often have wrong checksums because of NIC offloading; without <code>-C</code> Zeek drops those packets and logs can come out empty.</li>\n<li><code>local</code>: load the default site policy. It adds <code>software.log</code>, MD5/SHA1 hashes in <code>files.log</code> and certificate validation in <code>ssl.log</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<pre><code>conn.log  dhcp.log  dns.log  files.log  http.log  kerberos.log\nntlm.log  smb_files.log  smb_mapping.log  software.log  ssl.log  x509.log ...\n</code></pre>\n<h4 id=\"pcap-zeek-cut\">Reading Zeek logs with zeek-cut</h4>\n<p><code>zeek-cut</code> prints only the columns you name.</p>\n<pre><code>zeek-cut id.orig_h query &lt; zeek_out/dns.log\nzeek-cut -u ts id.orig_h host uri &lt; zeek_out/http.log\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><code>id.orig_h</code> / <code>id.orig_p</code>: the side that <strong>started</strong> the connection (usually the client).</li>\n<li><code>id.resp_h</code> / <code>id.resp_p</code>: the side that <strong>answered</strong> (usually the server).</li>\n<li><code>-u</code>: convert the epoch timestamp <code>ts</code> into a readable UTC date and time.</li>\n<li>Put <code>zeek-cut</code> <strong>before</strong> <code>grep</code>, not after. <code>zeek-cut</code> finds columns by reading the header lines at the top of the log; if <code>grep</code> runs first, the header is gone and <code>zeek-cut</code> prints nothing.</li>\n<li>Some field names changed between versions (for example <code>files.log</code> used <code>tx_hosts</code>/<code>rx_hosts</code>/<code>conn_uids</code> in older Bro/Zeek and <code>id.orig_h</code>/<code>id.resp_h</code>/<code>uid</code> in Zeek 6+). Check with <code>zeek --version</code> and <code>head -8 files.log</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"pcap-snort\">Generating Snort alerts</h3>\n<pre><code>mkdir snort_out\nsudo snort -q -k none -y -U -r case.pcap -c /etc/snort/snort.conf -A fast -l snort_out\ncat snort_out/alert\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><code>-q</code>: hide the start-up banner.</li>\n<li><code>-k none</code>: do not verify checksums (same reason as Zeek's <code>-C</code>).</li>\n<li><code>-y</code>: include the year in alert timestamps (fast alerts show only month/day otherwise).</li>\n<li><code>-U</code>: write timestamps in UTC instead of the analysis machine's time zone.</li>\n<li><code>-r case.pcap</code>: read the capture. <code>-c</code>: the config file that loads the rules.</li>\n<li><code>-A fast</code>: one line per alert. <code>-l snort_out</code>: write output to this folder.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"example-label\"><strong>Rules matter.</strong> Malware alerts (Emotet, IcedID, Trickbot, exploit kits…) only appear if <code>snort.conf</code> includes a current rule set such as <strong>ET Open</strong>. With only the default rules you may see nothing.</p>\n\n<p>One fast-alert line (fake example). The part after <code>{TCP}</code> is <code>source -&gt; destination</code>:</p>\n<pre><code>xx/xx/xx-xx:xx:xx.xxxxxx [**] [1:xxxxxxx:x] ET MALWARE ... CnC Beacon [**] [Classification: A Network Trojan was detected] [Priority: 1] {TCP} 172.xx.xx.xx:49xxx -&gt; x.x.x.x:8080\n</code></pre>\n<p>Useful one-liners:</p>\n<pre><code>grep -oE &quot;ET [A-Z_]+&quot; snort_out/alert | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn\ngrep -oE &quot;\\] ET [^\\[]*&quot; snort_out/alert | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn\ngrep -oE &quot;[0-9.]+:[0-9]+ -&gt; [0-9.]+:[0-9]+&quot; snort_out/alert | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn | head\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>Line 1: alert <strong>categories</strong> (<code>EXPLOIT_KIT</code>, <code>MALWARE</code>, <code>TROJAN</code>, <code>POLICY</code>, <code>CURRENT_EVENTS</code>…).</li>\n<li>Line 2: full alert <strong>names</strong>. <code>[^\\[]*</code> means \"everything up to the next <code>[</code>\".</li>\n<li>Line 3: the most frequent <code>source -&gt; destination</code> pairs.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"pcap-wireshark\">Wireshark essentials</h3>\n<p>Almost every tshark option has a GUI equivalent. Learn these four and every later section reads naturally.</p>\n<p><strong>① Display filter bar = tshark <code>-Y</code>.</strong> Type the filter in \"Apply a display filter…\" and press Enter. Green means valid, red means a syntax error. The text inside tshark's <code>-Y \"...\"</code> goes in unchanged.</p>\n<p><strong>② Apply as Column = tshark <code>-T fields -e</code>.</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Click a packet.</li>\n<li>In the Packet Details pane, expand ▶ until you see the field (for example <code>User-Agent</code>).</li>\n<li>Right-click it → <strong>Apply as Column</strong>. Clicking the column header sorts it, which also replaces <code>sort</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>③ Statistics menu = <code>sort | uniq -c</code>.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Statistics → Endpoints</strong>: packets per IP (<code>-z endpoints,ip</code>). Tabs for IPv4, TCP, Ethernet.</li>\n<li><strong>Statistics → Conversations</strong>: who talked to whom, with bytes and duration.</li>\n<li><strong>Statistics → HTTP → Requests</strong> and <strong>Request Sequences</strong>: hosts and URLs, and which page led to which request.</li>\n<li>Tick <strong>Limit to display filter</strong> at the bottom of Endpoints/Conversations to count only the packets your current filter shows. That is the same as \"<code>-Y</code> filter + <code>uniq -c</code>\".</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>④ Right-click → Apply as Filter → Selected.</strong> Builds a filter from any value in Packet Details, so you do not need to remember the syntax.</p>\n<p>Other features used in this lesson:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>View → Time Display Format → UTC Date and Time of Day</strong>: show real UTC times.</li>\n<li><strong>File → Export Objects → HTTP / SMB / TFTP / IMF</strong>: carve transferred files.</li>\n<li><strong>Right-click → Follow → TCP/HTTP Stream</strong>: read a whole conversation. Red is the client, blue is the server.</li>\n<li><strong>Edit → Find Packet</strong> (Ctrl+F) with <strong>String</strong> in <strong>Packet bytes</strong>: search inside payloads.</li>\n<li><strong>Right-click → Set/Unset Time Reference</strong>: make a packet \"time zero\" so later packets show +seconds.</li>\n<li><strong>Mark/Unmark Packet</strong> (Ctrl+M): bookmark packets you will cite in the report.</li>\n<li><strong>Edit → Configuration Profiles</strong>: create a profile such as \"IR-Lab\" so the extra columns do not clutter your normal layout.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"pcap-utc\">Time: always UTC</h3>\n<p>Answers use UTC. The same packet shows different local times on different analysis machines, so local times cause wrong answers.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>tshark: use the field <code>frame.time_utc</code>, or the option <code>-t ud</code>.</li>\n<li>Zeek: <code>zeek-cut -u</code>.</li>\n<li>Snort: <code>-U</code> (and <code>-y</code> for the year).</li>\n<li>Wireshark: <strong>View → Time Display Format → UTC Date and Time of Day</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"example-label\"><strong>Comparing times in awk.</strong> UTC timestamps in <code>YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SS</code> form sort the same way as text, so <code>awk -F'\\t' '$1 &gt; \"2019-01-01T10:00:00\"'</code> keeps only lines after that time.</p>\n\n<h3 id=\"pcap-timeline\">Building a timeline</h3>\n<p>The final written explanation of a compromise is a timeline in sentences. Pull the infected host's web activity in order:</p>\n<pre><code>tshark -r case.pcap -Y &quot;ip.src==&lt;infected IP&gt; &amp;&amp; (http.request || tls.handshake.type==1)&quot; \\\n  -T fields -e frame.time_utc -e ip.dst -e tcp.dstport -e http.host -e http.request.uri \\\n  -e tls.handshake.extensions_server_name\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><code>tls.handshake.type==1</code>: only the first packet of each HTTPS connection (Client Hello), so HTTPS destinations appear in time order next to HTTP requests.</li>\n<li><code>tls.handshake.extensions_server_name</code>: the domain (SNI) the client asked for.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Wireshark: set UTC time, apply the same filter, and read Host and Server Name columns top to bottom. Mark the key packets as you go.</p>", "ko": "<div class=\"example-label\" markdown=\"0\"><strong>이 절의 범위.</strong> <ul>\n<li>대부분의 조사 질문에 답할 수 있는 고정된 작업 순서</li>\n<li>0단계: <code>capinfos</code>로 파일 훑어보기</li>\n<li>PCAP을 Zeek 로그와 Snort 2 알림으로 바꾸고, <code>zeek-cut</code>과 <code>grep</code>으로 읽기</li>\n<li>tshark 옵션 대부분을 대신하는 Wireshark 기본 기술 4가지</li>\n<li>답을 왜 UTC로 쓰는지, 타임라인 만드는 법</li>\n</ul></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"pcap-workflow\">표준 분석 순서</h3>\n<p>PCAP 조사는 대부분 같은 이야기입니다. 윈도우(또는 다른) PC 한 대가 받으면 안 되는 것을 받았고, 누가·무엇을·언제·어디서 받았고 그다음 무슨 일이 있었는지를 증명해야 합니다. 질문을 먼저 읽기보다 매번 같은 순서로 작업하는 편이 빠릅니다.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>0 · capinfos — time range, size</li>\n<li>1 · Zeek + Snort — logs and alerts</li>\n<li>2 · Suspect IP — alerts, exe/doc receiver</li>\n<li>3 · Identify the host — MAC, hostname, user, OS</li>\n<li>4 · Downloads — URLs, sizes, extract, hash</li>\n<li>5 · Classify — alert names, hash lookup</li>\n<li>6 · After the download — C2, spread to DC</li>\n<li>7 · Timeline in UTC</li>\n<li>8 · Short written answer</li>\n</ol>\n<p>위에서 아래로 읽습니다. 2~3단계는 \"누가\", 4단계는 \"무엇을 어디서\", 5~6단계는 \"그게 무엇이고 무엇을 했나\"에 답하고, 7단계에서 모두 시간순으로 정리해 마지막 설명 문제에 씁니다.</p>\n<h3 id=\"pcap-capinfos\">0단계: 파일 훑어보기</h3>\n<pre><code>capinfos case.pcap\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><code>capinfos</code>: Wireshark에 포함된 도구로, 패킷 수와 첫·마지막 패킷 시간, 크기를 요약해 줍니다.</li>\n</ul>\n<pre><code>Number of packets:   xx k\nFirst packet time:   ...\nLast packet time:    ...\n</code></pre>\n<p>Wireshark: <strong>Statistics → Capture File Properties</strong>(같은 정보), <strong>Statistics → Protocol Hierarchy</strong>(HTTP, TLS, Kerberos, DHCP, SMB가 각각 얼마나 있는지).</p>\n<h3 id=\"pcap-zeek\">Zeek 로그 만들기</h3>\n<pre><code>mkdir zeek_out &amp;&amp; cd zeek_out\nzeek -C -r ../case.pcap local\nls *.log\ncd ..\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><code>-r ../case.pcap</code>: 실시간 네트워크가 아니라 이 파일을 읽습니다.</li>\n<li><code>-C</code>: 체크섬 오류를 무시합니다. 캡처할 때 네트워크 카드 오프로딩 때문에 체크섬이 틀리게 찍히는 경우가 많고, <code>-C</code>가 없으면 Zeek가 그 패킷을 버려 로그가 비어 나올 수 있습니다.</li>\n<li><code>local</code>: 기본 분석 정책을 불러옵니다. <code>software.log</code>, <code>files.log</code>의 MD5·SHA1, <code>ssl.log</code>의 인증서 검증 결과가 이 옵션으로 생깁니다.</li>\n</ul>\n<pre><code>conn.log  dhcp.log  dns.log  files.log  http.log  kerberos.log\nntlm.log  smb_files.log  smb_mapping.log  software.log  ssl.log  x509.log ...\n</code></pre>\n<h4 id=\"pcap-zeek-cut\">zeek-cut으로 Zeek 로그 읽기</h4>\n<p><code>zeek-cut</code>은 이름을 적은 칸만 출력합니다.</p>\n<pre><code>zeek-cut id.orig_h query &lt; zeek_out/dns.log\nzeek-cut -u ts id.orig_h host uri &lt; zeek_out/http.log\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><code>id.orig_h</code> / <code>id.orig_p</code>: 연결을 <strong>시작한</strong> 쪽(보통 클라이언트)의 IP와 포트.</li>\n<li><code>id.resp_h</code> / <code>id.resp_p</code>: <strong>응답한</strong> 쪽(보통 서버)의 IP와 포트.</li>\n<li><code>-u</code>: 숫자 시간(<code>ts</code>, epoch)을 읽기 쉬운 UTC 날짜·시각으로 바꿉니다.</li>\n<li><code>zeek-cut</code>을 <code>grep</code> <strong>앞에</strong> 씁니다. <code>zeek-cut</code>은 로그 맨 위의 칸 이름 줄(헤더)을 보고 칸을 찾는데, <code>grep</code>이 먼저 오면 헤더가 사라져 아무것도 출력하지 못합니다.</li>\n<li>버전마다 칸 이름이 조금 다릅니다(예: 예전 Bro/Zeek의 <code>files.log</code>는 <code>tx_hosts</code>/<code>rx_hosts</code>/<code>conn_uids</code>, Zeek 6 이상은 <code>id.orig_h</code>/<code>id.resp_h</code>/<code>uid</code>). <code>zeek --version</code>과 <code>head -8 files.log</code>로 확인합니다.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"pcap-snort\">Snort 알림 만들기</h3>\n<pre><code>mkdir snort_out\nsudo snort -q -k none -y -U -r case.pcap -c /etc/snort/snort.conf -A fast -l snort_out\ncat snort_out/alert\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><code>-q</code>: 시작 안내문을 숨깁니다.</li>\n<li><code>-k none</code>: 체크섬 검사를 끕니다(Zeek의 <code>-C</code>와 같은 이유).</li>\n<li><code>-y</code>: 알림 시각에 연도를 넣습니다(없으면 월/일만 나옴).</li>\n<li><code>-U</code>: 분석 PC의 시간대 대신 UTC로 시각을 찍습니다.</li>\n<li><code>-r case.pcap</code>: 캡처 파일을 읽습니다. <code>-c</code>: 규칙을 불러오는 설정 파일.</li>\n<li><code>-A fast</code>: 알림을 한 줄씩. <code>-l snort_out</code>: 결과를 이 폴더에 저장.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"example-label\"><strong>규칙이 있어야 잡힙니다.</strong> 악성코드 알림(Emotet, IcedID, Trickbot, 익스플로잇 킷 등)은 <code>snort.conf</code>에 <strong>ET Open</strong> 같은 최신 룰셋이 들어 있어야 나옵니다. 기본 규칙만 있으면 아무것도 안 나올 수 있습니다.</p>\n\n<p>fast 알림 한 줄(가짜 예시)입니다. <code>{TCP}</code> 뒤가 <code>출발지 -&gt; 목적지</code>입니다.</p>\n<pre><code>xx/xx/xx-xx:xx:xx.xxxxxx [**] [1:xxxxxxx:x] ET MALWARE ... CnC Beacon [**] [Classification: A Network Trojan was detected] [Priority: 1] {TCP} 172.xx.xx.xx:49xxx -&gt; x.x.x.x:8080\n</code></pre>\n<p>자주 쓰는 한 줄 명령:</p>\n<pre><code>grep -oE &quot;ET [A-Z_]+&quot; snort_out/alert | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn\ngrep -oE &quot;\\] ET [^\\[]*&quot; snort_out/alert | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn\ngrep -oE &quot;[0-9.]+:[0-9]+ -&gt; [0-9.]+:[0-9]+&quot; snort_out/alert | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn | head\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>1줄: 알림 <strong>분류</strong>(<code>EXPLOIT_KIT</code>, <code>MALWARE</code>, <code>TROJAN</code>, <code>POLICY</code>, <code>CURRENT_EVENTS</code> 등)별 개수.</li>\n<li>2줄: 알림 <strong>이름</strong> 전체. <code>[^\\[]*</code>는 \"다음 <code>[</code>가 나오기 전까지 전부\"라는 뜻입니다.</li>\n<li>3줄: 가장 자주 나온 <code>출발지 -&gt; 목적지</code> 쌍.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"pcap-wireshark\">Wireshark 기본 기술</h3>\n<p>tshark 옵션은 대부분 Wireshark 화면에 같은 기능이 있습니다. 아래 네 가지를 익히면 뒤 절들이 쉽게 읽힙니다.</p>\n<p><strong>① 화면 필터 바 = tshark <code>-Y</code>.</strong> \"Apply a display filter…\" 칸에 필터를 쓰고 Enter를 누릅니다. 초록색은 맞는 문법, 빨간색은 틀린 문법입니다. tshark의 <code>-Y \"...\"</code> 따옴표 안 내용을 그대로 넣으면 됩니다.</p>\n<p><strong>② Apply as Column = tshark <code>-T fields -e</code>.</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>패킷 하나를 클릭합니다.</li>\n<li>가운데 상세 창(Packet Details)에서 ▶를 펼쳐 원하는 항목(예: <code>User-Agent</code>)을 찾습니다.</li>\n<li>우클릭 → <strong>Apply as Column</strong>. 열 제목을 클릭하면 정렬되므로 <code>sort</code> 역할도 합니다.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>③ Statistics 메뉴 = <code>sort | uniq -c</code>.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Statistics → Endpoints</strong>: IP별 패킷 수(<code>-z endpoints,ip</code>). IPv4, TCP, Ethernet 탭.</li>\n<li><strong>Statistics → Conversations</strong>: 누가 누구와 통신했는지, 바이트와 지속 시간까지.</li>\n<li><strong>Statistics → HTTP → Requests</strong>와 <strong>Request Sequences</strong>: 접속한 호스트와 URL, 어느 페이지가 어느 요청으로 이어졌는지.</li>\n<li>Endpoints·Conversations 창 아래의 <strong>Limit to display filter</strong>를 체크하면 지금 필터에 걸린 패킷만 셉니다. \"<code>-Y</code> 필터 + <code>uniq -c</code>\"와 같은 효과입니다.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>④ 우클릭 → Apply as Filter → Selected.</strong> 상세 창의 아무 값이나 필터로 만들어 줍니다. 문법을 외우지 않아도 됩니다.</p>\n<p>이 강의에서 함께 쓰는 기능:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>View → Time Display Format → UTC Date and Time of Day</strong>: 실제 UTC 시각 표시.</li>\n<li><strong>File → Export Objects → HTTP / SMB / TFTP / IMF</strong>: 오간 파일 꺼내기.</li>\n<li><strong>우클릭 → Follow → TCP/HTTP Stream</strong>: 대화 전체 읽기. 빨간색은 클라이언트, 파란색은 서버.</li>\n<li><strong>Edit → Find Packet</strong>(Ctrl+F), <strong>String</strong>, <strong>Packet bytes</strong>: 패킷 내용 안에서 글자 찾기.</li>\n<li><strong>우클릭 → Set/Unset Time Reference</strong>: 그 패킷을 기준 시점(0초)으로 만들어 이후 패킷이 +초로 보이게 합니다.</li>\n<li><strong>Mark/Unmark Packet</strong>(Ctrl+M): 보고서에 쓸 패킷 표시.</li>\n<li><strong>Edit → Configuration Profiles</strong>: \"IR-Lab\" 같은 프로필을 만들어 추가한 열이 평소 화면을 어지럽히지 않게 합니다.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"pcap-utc\">시간은 항상 UTC</h3>\n<p>답안 시각은 UTC로 씁니다. 같은 패킷도 분석 PC의 시간대에 따라 현지 시각이 다르게 보여 오답이 나옵니다.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>tshark: <code>frame.time_utc</code> 필드, 또는 <code>-t ud</code> 옵션.</li>\n<li>Zeek: <code>zeek-cut -u</code>.</li>\n<li>Snort: <code>-U</code>(연도는 <code>-y</code>).</li>\n<li>Wireshark: <strong>View → Time Display Format → UTC Date and Time of Day</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"example-label\"><strong>awk로 시각 비교하기.</strong> <code>YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SS</code> 형태의 UTC 시각은 글자 순서와 시간 순서가 같아서, <code>awk -F'\\t' '$1 &gt; \"2019-01-01T10:00:00\"'</code>처럼 쓰면 그 시각 이후 줄만 남습니다.</p>\n\n<h3 id=\"pcap-timeline\">타임라인 만들기</h3>\n<p>침해를 설명하는 마지막 보고 문장은 문장으로 쓴 타임라인입니다. 감염 호스트의 웹 활동을 시간순으로 뽑습니다.</p>\n<pre><code>tshark -r case.pcap -Y &quot;ip.src==&lt;infected IP&gt; &amp;&amp; (http.request || tls.handshake.type==1)&quot; \\\n  -T fields -e frame.time_utc -e ip.dst -e tcp.dstport -e http.host -e http.request.uri \\\n  -e tls.handshake.extensions_server_name\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><code>tls.handshake.type==1</code>: HTTPS 연결마다 첫 패킷(Client Hello)만 골라, HTTP 요청 옆에 HTTPS 목적지도 시간순으로 보이게 합니다.</li>\n<li><code>tls.handshake.extensions_server_name</code>: 클라이언트가 요청한 도메인(SNI).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Wireshark: UTC 시각으로 바꾸고, 같은 필터를 넣고, Host와 Server Name 열을 위에서 아래로 읽습니다. 중요한 패킷은 그때그때 Mark 해 둡니다.</p>"}}, {"id": "host-identification", "title": {"en": "Identifying hosts", "ko": "호스트 신원 확인"}, "html": {"en": "<div class=\"example-label\" markdown=\"0\"><strong>Scope of this section.</strong> <ul>\n<li>Finding the infected host from alerts and downloads</li>\n<li>IP, MAC, hostname and Windows user account</li>\n<li>Finding the Domain Controller and counting active hosts</li>\n<li>Fingerprinting devices (Ubuntu, Android, Amazon Fire, Apple, smart TVs) and the operating system</li>\n<li>Key network information (IP range, gateway, domain) and the host's public IP</li>\n</ul></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"pcap-infected-host\">Finding the infected host</h3>\n<p>The infected host is the internal IP that appears in the malware alerts <strong>and</strong> received the suspicious file. Check both and make sure they point to the same address.</p>\n<pre><code>grep -oE &quot;\\{(TCP|UDP)\\} [0-9.]+:[0-9]+ -&gt; [0-9.]+:[0-9]+&quot; snort_out/alert | grep -oE &quot;(10|172|192)\\.[0-9.]+&quot; | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn | head\nzeek-cut -u ts id.orig_h host uri resp_mime_types &lt; zeek_out/http.log | grep -i -E &quot;dosexec|msword|officedocument|ms-office&quot;\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>Line 1: take <code>{TCP} source -&gt; destination</code> from each alert, keep the private-looking addresses, count them. (The regex also matches some public ranges such as <code>172.217.x.x</code>, so read the list, do not trust it blindly.)</li>\n<li>Line 2: who requested something that came back as a Windows executable (<code>application/x-dosexec</code>) or a Word document. <code>id.orig_h</code> is the requester.</li>\n</ul>\n<pre><code>     57 172.xx.xx.xx\n2019-xx-xxTxx:xx:xx  172.xx.xx.xx  &lt;site&gt;  /&lt;path&gt;/&lt;file&gt;.doc  application/msword\n</code></pre>\n<p>Wireshark: <strong>Statistics → Endpoints → IPv4</strong> for the internal addresses, then <strong>File → Export Objects → HTTP</strong>; click a suspicious file's <strong>Packet</strong> number and read the Destination column (the response goes <em>to</em> the client). The filter <code>http.response &amp;&amp; http.file_data contains \"This program cannot be run in DOS mode\"</code> jumps straight to responses that carry a Windows executable.</p>\n<h3 id=\"pcap-ip-mac-hostname\">IP, MAC and hostname</h3>\n<p>DHCP gives IP, MAC and hostname together, so start there. If there is no DHCP in the capture, use the host's own packets and its name announcements.</p>\n<pre><code>zeek-cut client_addr mac host_name &lt; zeek_out/dhcp.log | sort -u\ntshark -r case.pcap -Y &quot;dhcp&quot; -T fields -e ip.src -e eth.src -e dhcp.option.hostname -e dhcp.option.vendor_class_id | sort -u\ntshark -r case.pcap -Y &quot;ip.src==&lt;infected IP&gt;&quot; -T fields -e eth.src | sort -u\ntshark -r case.pcap -Y &quot;(nbns || mdns || llmnr) &amp;&amp; ip.src==&lt;infected IP&gt;&quot; -T fields -e nbns.name -e dns.qry.name | sort -u\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>Lines 1–2: DHCP records. In older Wireshark, use <code>bootp</code> instead of <code>dhcp</code>.</li>\n<li>Line 3: the MAC address on the host's own packets.</li>\n<li>Line 4: names the host announces: NBNS/LLMNR on Windows (<code>DESKTOP-XXXX&lt;00&gt;</code>), mDNS on Apple/Linux/Android (<code>&lt;name&gt;.local</code>).</li>\n<li>A Kerberos computer account ending in <code>$</code> (next section) is another reliable hostname source.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Wireshark:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Filter <code>dhcp</code> → click a <strong>Request</strong> → expand <strong>Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol</strong> → right-click <strong>Option (12) Host Name</strong> → <strong>Apply as Column</strong>; do the same for <strong>Option (60) Vendor class identifier</strong>.</li>\n<li>Filter <code>ip.src==&lt;infected IP&gt;</code> → <strong>Ethernet II → Source</strong> is the MAC.</li>\n<li>Filter <code>nbns</code> → the Info column shows <code>Registration NB &lt;NAME&gt;&lt;00&gt;</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"pcap-user-account\">Windows user account</h3>\n<p>When a Windows PC logs on to the domain, Kerberos carries the name that logged on. Names ending in <code>$</code> are <strong>computer</strong> accounts; names without <code>$</code> are <strong>user</strong> accounts.</p>\n<pre><code>tshark -r case.pcap -Y &quot;kerberos.CNameString &amp;&amp; ip.src==&lt;infected IP&gt;&quot; -T fields -e kerberos.CNameString -e kerberos.crealm | sort | uniq -c\nzeek-cut id.orig_h client &lt; zeek_out/kerberos.log | sort -u\n</code></pre>\n<pre><code>      8 DESKTOP-XXXXXXX$   EXAMPLE.LOCAL     ← computer account (hostname)\n      5 &lt;username&gt;         EXAMPLE.LOCAL     ← user account\n</code></pre>\n<p>Wireshark: filter <code>kerberos.CNameString &amp;&amp; !(kerberos.CNameString contains \"$\")</code> → expand <strong>Kerberos → as-req → req-body → cname → cname-string → CNameString</strong> → <strong>Apply as Column</strong>. <code>!</code> means \"not\", so only user names remain. Write the name exactly as it appears, including case.</p>\n<h3 id=\"pcap-domain-controller\">Finding the Domain Controller</h3>\n<p>A DC answers Kerberos (88), LDAP (389), DNS (53) and SMB (445). Count which IP receives Kerberos and LDAP requests.</p>\n<pre><code>tshark -r case.pcap -Y &quot;tcp.dstport==88 || udp.dstport==88 || tcp.dstport==389 || udp.dstport==389&quot; -T fields -e ip.dst | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn\nzeek-cut id.orig_h id.resp_h service &lt; zeek_out/kerberos.log | sort -u | head\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>Line 1 usually shows one IP: the DC.</li>\n<li>Line 2: the <code>service</code> column shows <code>krbtgt/&lt;DOMAIN&gt;</code> and <code>LDAP/&lt;dc-name&gt;.&lt;domain&gt;</code>, which also gives the DC's hostname.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Wireshark: apply the line-1 filter → <strong>Statistics → Endpoints → IPv4</strong> with <strong>Limit to display filter</strong> → sort by <strong>Rx Packets</strong>. For the DC name, filter <code>kerberos</code>, click a <code>TGS-REQ</code> and expand <strong>req-body → sname</strong>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"pcap-active-hosts\">Active hosts on the LAN</h3>\n<p>\"Active\" means an internal IP that <strong>started</strong> connections.</p>\n<pre><code>zeek-cut id.orig_h &lt; zeek_out/conn.log | grep '^172\\.16\\.' | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn\ntshark -r case.pcap -q -z endpoints,ip | grep &quot;172.16.&quot;\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>Adjust the prefix to your network's subnet. <code>\\.</code> means a literal dot.</li>\n<li>Remove from the count: the DC (if the question says \"besides the DC\"), the broadcast address (<code>.255</code>), and multicast addresses (<code>224.x</code>, <code>239.255.255.250</code>). The default gateway usually appears only in ARP, not as an IP talker.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Wireshark: filter <code>ip.src==172.16.0.0/12</code> (or your network's range) → <strong>Statistics → Endpoints → IPv4</strong> → <strong>Limit to display filter</strong> → IPs with <strong>Tx Packets</strong> above zero.</p>\n<h3 id=\"pcap-device-fingerprinting\">Fingerprinting devices</h3>\n<p>To say what a device is, combine three clues. One clue alone can mislead.</p>\n<div class=\"article-table-wrap\"><table class=\"article-table\">\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Clue</th>\n<th>How to get it</th>\n<th>What it tells you</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>MAC vendor (first 3 bytes, OUI)</td>\n<td><code>-e eth.src -e eth.src_resolved</code>; <strong>Statistics → Endpoints → Ethernet</strong> with <strong>Name resolution</strong></td>\n<td>Manufacturer: Apple, Samsung, Amazon Technologies…</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>DHCP vendor class (option 60) and hostname (option 12)</td>\n<td>DHCP commands above</td>\n<td><code>MSFT 5.0</code> = Windows, <code>android-dhcp-8.0.0</code> = Android 8.0.0, <code>dhcpcd-…</code> = Linux</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>DNS queries and HTTP User-Agent</td>\n<td>commands below</td>\n<td>What services it talks to and what software it runs</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<pre><code>zeek-cut id.orig_h query &lt; zeek_out/dns.log | awk '$1==&quot;&lt;IP&gt;&quot;' | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn | head -30\nzeek-cut id.orig_h host user_agent &lt; zeek_out/http.log | awk -F'\\t' '$1==&quot;&lt;IP&gt;&quot;' | sort -u\nzeek-cut host software_type name unparsed_version &lt; zeek_out/software.log | sort -u\ntshark -r case.pcap -Y 'ip.src==&lt;IP&gt; &amp;&amp; (mdns || ssdp)' -V | grep -i -E &quot;server:|\\.local|_tcp&quot; | sort -u | head -40\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><code>awk -F'\\t'</code>: split on tabs, because User-Agent strings contain spaces.</li>\n<li><code>software.log</code>: Zeek's summary of software seen in User-Agents and banners.</li>\n<li>Last line: TVs, streaming sticks and printers announce themselves over mDNS and SSDP. The SSDP <code>SERVER:</code> header and mDNS service names (<code>_googlecast._tcp.local</code>, <code>_airplay._tcp.local</code>) name the device type.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Typical signs:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Ubuntu</strong>: DNS to <code>connectivity-check.ubuntu.com</code>, <code>ntp.ubuntu.com</code>, <code>api.snapcraft.io</code>, <code>archive/security.ubuntu.com</code>; User-Agent containing <code>Ubuntu</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Android</strong>: <code>connectivitycheck.gstatic.com</code>, <code>android.clients.google.com</code>; User-Agent <code>Dalvik/2.1.0 (Linux; U; Android 8.0.0; &lt;model code&gt; Build/&lt;build&gt;)</code>. The model code (for example one starting <code>SM-</code> for Samsung) gives brand and model when you search it.</li>\n<li><strong>Amazon Fire tablet</strong>: many <code>amazon</code> domains, User-Agent containing <code>Silk</code> or a model code starting <code>KF</code>, MAC vendor Amazon Technologies. Echo speakers also talk to Amazon, so check User-Agent and MAC too.</li>\n<li><strong>Apple</strong>: <code>apple.com</code>, <code>icloud.com</code>, <code>push.apple.com</code>; User-Agent <code>iPhone</code> or <code>Macintosh</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Wireshark filters: <code>dns.qry.name contains \"ubuntu\"</code>, <code>http.user_agent contains \"Android 8.0.0\"</code>, <code>dns.qry.name contains \"amazon\" || http.user_agent contains \"Silk\"</code>, <code>ip.src==&lt;IP&gt; &amp;&amp; dns.flags.response==0</code> (the host's DNS questions only).</p>\n<h3 id=\"pcap-operating-system\">Operating system</h3>\n<p>When the question does not say the host is Windows, collect two or three pieces of evidence for the OS.</p>\n<pre><code>tshark -r case.pcap -Y &quot;ip.src==&lt;IP&gt;&quot; -T fields -e ip.ttl | sort | uniq -c\ntshark -r case.pcap -Y &quot;ntlmssp.version.major &amp;&amp; ip.src==&lt;IP&gt;&quot; -T fields -e ntlmssp.version.major -e ntlmssp.version.minor -e ntlmssp.version.build_number | sort -u\ntshark -r case.pcap -Y &quot;smb.native_os &amp;&amp; ip.src==&lt;IP&gt;&quot; -T fields -e smb.native_os | sort -u\ntshark -r case.pcap -Y &quot;ssh.protocol&quot; -T fields -e ip.src -e ssh.protocol | sort -u\np0f -r case.pcap -o p0f.log &amp;&amp; grep &quot;&lt;IP&gt;&quot; p0f.log | grep os=\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>TTL</strong>: starting values differ by OS. Windows usually starts at 128, Linux/macOS/Android at 64. Captured on the same LAN, you see the starting value.</li>\n<li><strong>NTLMSSP version</strong>: NTLM authentication carries the Windows version. <code>10 0 19045</code> = Windows 10 22H2, <code>6 1 7601</code> = Windows 7 SP1.</li>\n<li><strong>smb.native_os</strong>: SMB1 hosts announce their OS name.</li>\n<li><strong>SSH banner</strong>: may include <code>Ubuntu</code> or <code>Debian</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>User-Agent</strong>: <code>Windows NT 10.0</code> = Windows 10/11, <code>Windows NT 6.1</code> = Windows 7, <code>X11; Linux x86_64</code> = Linux.</li>\n<li><strong>p0f</strong>: passive OS fingerprinting from TCP SYN packets.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Wireshark: add <strong>Internet Protocol → Time to Live</strong> as a column; filter <code>ntlmssp.version.major</code> and expand <strong>NTLM Secure Service Provider → Version</strong>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"pcap-network-info\">Key network information</h3>\n<p>DHCP ACKs and Kerberos describe the network itself.</p>\n<pre><code>tshark -r case.pcap -Y &quot;dhcp.option.dhcp == 5&quot; -T fields -e dhcp.option.subnet_mask -e dhcp.option.router -e dhcp.option.domain_name_server -e dhcp.option.domain_name | sort -u\ntshark -r case.pcap -Y &quot;kerberos&quot; -T fields -e kerberos.realm -e kerberos.crealm | tr '\\t' '\\n' | sort -u\ntshark -r case.pcap -Y 'dns.qry.name contains &quot;_msdcs&quot;' -T fields -e ip.src -e dns.qry.name | sort -u\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><code>dhcp.option.dhcp == 5</code>: DHCP ACK only. Subnet mask <code>255.255.255.0</code> = a /24; router = default gateway; DNS server is usually the DC; domain name.</li>\n<li>Kerberos realm = the AD domain.</li>\n<li><code>_ldap._tcp.dc._msdcs.&lt;domain&gt;</code>: Windows clients looking for the DC also reveal the domain.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Wireshark: filter <code>dhcp.option.dhcp == 5</code> and read options (1) Subnet Mask, (3) Router, (6) Domain Name Server, (15) Domain Name; filter <code>kerberos</code>, open an <strong>AS-REQ</strong> and read <strong>req-body → realm</strong>. </p>\n<h3 id=\"pcap-public-ip\">Public IP</h3>\n<p>The host uses a private IP inside; the firewall or router translates it (NAT) to a public IP. A capture taken inside never shows the public IP in packet headers. But malware often asks an \"what is my IP\" service right after infection, and the <strong>response body</strong> contains the answer.</p>\n<pre><code>zeek-cut -u ts id.orig_h host uri &lt; zeek_out/http.log | grep -i -E &quot;ip|myip|whatismy|ifconfig&quot;\nzeek-cut -u ts query answers &lt; zeek_out/dns.log | grep -i -E &quot;ipify|checkip|ip-api|icanhazip|myexternalip|ipinfo|wtfismyip|ifconfig&quot;\ntshark -r case.pcap -Y 'http.response &amp;&amp; http.response_for.uri contains &quot;&lt;ip-check domain&gt;&quot;' -T fields -e frame.number -e http.file_data\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><code>http.file_data</code>: the HTTP response body, for example <code>x.x.x.x</code> or <code>{\"ip\":\"x.x.x.x\"}</code>. If it prints as hex, append <code>| xxd -r -p</code>.</li>\n<li>If the IP check used HTTPS, the body is encrypted. Look for the IP inside other plaintext traffic (for example a C2 POST), or state that it cannot be determined from the PCAP and why.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Wireshark: filter <code>http.host contains \"ip\" || dns.qry.name contains \"ip\"</code> → right-click the request → <strong>Follow → HTTP Stream</strong> → the blue (server) body shows the IP. Or open <strong>Line-based text data</strong> / <strong>JavaScript Object Notation</strong> in Packet Details.</p>", "ko": "<div class=\"example-label\" markdown=\"0\"><strong>이 절의 범위.</strong> <ul>\n<li>알림과 다운로드로 감염 호스트 찾기</li>\n<li>IP, MAC, 호스트 이름, 윈도우 사용자 계정</li>\n<li>도메인 컨트롤러 찾기와 활동 호스트 세기</li>\n<li>기기 판별(Ubuntu, 안드로이드, 아마존 Fire, 애플, 스마트 TV)과 운영체제</li>\n<li>핵심 네트워크 정보(IP 대역, 게이트웨이, 도메인)와 호스트의 공인 IP</li>\n</ul></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"pcap-infected-host\">감염 호스트 찾기</h3>\n<p>감염 호스트는 악성코드 알림에 나오면서 <strong>동시에</strong> 수상한 파일을 받은 내부 IP입니다. 둘 다 확인해서 같은 주소를 가리키는지 봅니다.</p>\n<pre><code>grep -oE &quot;\\{(TCP|UDP)\\} [0-9.]+:[0-9]+ -&gt; [0-9.]+:[0-9]+&quot; snort_out/alert | grep -oE &quot;(10|172|192)\\.[0-9.]+&quot; | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn | head\nzeek-cut -u ts id.orig_h host uri resp_mime_types &lt; zeek_out/http.log | grep -i -E &quot;dosexec|msword|officedocument|ms-office&quot;\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>1줄: 알림마다 <code>{TCP} 출발지 -&gt; 목적지</code> 부분을 잘라 사설 주소처럼 보이는 것만 남기고 셉니다. (<code>172.217.x.x</code> 같은 공인 대역도 걸리므로 목록을 눈으로 확인합니다.)</li>\n<li>2줄: 윈도우 실행파일(<code>application/x-dosexec</code>)이나 Word 문서를 받아 간 요청. <code>id.orig_h</code>가 요청한 쪽입니다.</li>\n</ul>\n<pre><code>     57 172.xx.xx.xx\n2019-xx-xxTxx:xx:xx  172.xx.xx.xx  &lt;site&gt;  /&lt;path&gt;/&lt;file&gt;.doc  application/msword\n</code></pre>\n<p>Wireshark: <strong>Statistics → Endpoints → IPv4</strong>로 내부 주소를 보고, <strong>File → Export Objects → HTTP</strong>에서 수상한 파일의 <strong>Packet</strong> 번호를 클릭해 Destination 열을 읽습니다(응답은 클라이언트 <em>쪽으로</em> 갑니다). 필터 <code>http.response &amp;&amp; http.file_data contains \"This program cannot be run in DOS mode\"</code>는 윈도우 실행파일이 담긴 응답으로 바로 데려갑니다.</p>\n<h3 id=\"pcap-ip-mac-hostname\">IP, MAC, 호스트 이름</h3>\n<p>DHCP에는 IP·MAC·호스트 이름이 함께 있으니 거기서 시작합니다. 캡처에 DHCP가 없으면 호스트가 보낸 패킷과 이름 알림을 봅니다.</p>\n<pre><code>zeek-cut client_addr mac host_name &lt; zeek_out/dhcp.log | sort -u\ntshark -r case.pcap -Y &quot;dhcp&quot; -T fields -e ip.src -e eth.src -e dhcp.option.hostname -e dhcp.option.vendor_class_id | sort -u\ntshark -r case.pcap -Y &quot;ip.src==&lt;infected IP&gt;&quot; -T fields -e eth.src | sort -u\ntshark -r case.pcap -Y &quot;(nbns || mdns || llmnr) &amp;&amp; ip.src==&lt;infected IP&gt;&quot; -T fields -e nbns.name -e dns.qry.name | sort -u\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>1~2줄: DHCP 기록. 옛날 Wireshark에서는 <code>dhcp</code> 대신 <code>bootp</code>를 씁니다.</li>\n<li>3줄: 호스트가 보낸 패킷의 MAC 주소.</li>\n<li>4줄: 호스트가 스스로 알리는 이름. 윈도우는 NBNS·LLMNR(<code>DESKTOP-XXXX&lt;00&gt;</code>), 애플·리눅스·안드로이드는 mDNS(<code>&lt;이름&gt;.local</code>).</li>\n<li>다음 절의 Kerberos 컴퓨터 계정(끝이 <code>$</code>)도 믿을 만한 호스트 이름 출처입니다.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Wireshark:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>필터 <code>dhcp</code> → <strong>Request</strong> 클릭 → <strong>Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol</strong> 펼치기 → <strong>Option (12) Host Name</strong> 우클릭 → <strong>Apply as Column</strong>. <strong>Option (60) Vendor class identifier</strong>도 같은 방법으로 추가합니다.</li>\n<li>필터 <code>ip.src==&lt;infected IP&gt;</code> → <strong>Ethernet II → Source</strong>가 MAC입니다.</li>\n<li>필터 <code>nbns</code> → Info 열에 <code>Registration NB &lt;이름&gt;&lt;00&gt;</code>이 보입니다.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"pcap-user-account\">윈도우 사용자 계정</h3>\n<p>윈도우 PC가 도메인에 로그인하면 Kerberos에 로그인한 이름이 실립니다. <code>$</code>로 끝나면 <strong>컴퓨터</strong> 계정, <code>$</code>가 없으면 <strong>사용자</strong> 계정입니다.</p>\n<pre><code>tshark -r case.pcap -Y &quot;kerberos.CNameString &amp;&amp; ip.src==&lt;infected IP&gt;&quot; -T fields -e kerberos.CNameString -e kerberos.crealm | sort | uniq -c\nzeek-cut id.orig_h client &lt; zeek_out/kerberos.log | sort -u\n</code></pre>\n<pre><code>      8 DESKTOP-XXXXXXX$   EXAMPLE.LOCAL     ← 컴퓨터 계정(호스트 이름)\n      5 &lt;username&gt;         EXAMPLE.LOCAL     ← 사용자 계정\n</code></pre>\n<p>Wireshark: 필터 <code>kerberos.CNameString &amp;&amp; !(kerberos.CNameString contains \"$\")</code> → <strong>Kerberos → as-req → req-body → cname → cname-string → CNameString</strong> 펼치기 → <strong>Apply as Column</strong>. <code>!</code>는 \"아닌 것\"이라 사용자 이름만 남습니다. 답안에는 대소문자까지 보이는 그대로 씁니다.</p>\n<h3 id=\"pcap-domain-controller\">도메인 컨트롤러 찾기</h3>\n<p>DC는 Kerberos(88), LDAP(389), DNS(53), SMB(445)에 응답합니다. Kerberos·LDAP 요청을 받는 IP를 셉니다.</p>\n<pre><code>tshark -r case.pcap -Y &quot;tcp.dstport==88 || udp.dstport==88 || tcp.dstport==389 || udp.dstport==389&quot; -T fields -e ip.dst | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn\nzeek-cut id.orig_h id.resp_h service &lt; zeek_out/kerberos.log | sort -u | head\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>1줄: 보통 IP 하나만 나오며 그게 DC입니다.</li>\n<li>2줄: <code>service</code> 칸의 <code>krbtgt/&lt;도메인&gt;</code>, <code>LDAP/&lt;dc이름&gt;.&lt;도메인&gt;</code>에서 DC 호스트 이름까지 알 수 있습니다.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Wireshark: 1줄의 필터 입력 → <strong>Statistics → Endpoints → IPv4</strong>에서 <strong>Limit to display filter</strong> 체크 → <strong>Rx Packets</strong>로 정렬. DC 이름은 필터 <code>kerberos</code> → <code>TGS-REQ</code> 클릭 → <strong>req-body → sname</strong>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"pcap-active-hosts\">LAN의 활동 호스트</h3>\n<p>\"활동 중\"은 스스로 연결을 <strong>시작한</strong> 내부 IP라는 뜻입니다.</p>\n<pre><code>zeek-cut id.orig_h &lt; zeek_out/conn.log | grep '^172\\.16\\.' | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn\ntshark -r case.pcap -q -z endpoints,ip | grep &quot;172.16.&quot;\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>앞자리는 조사하는 네트워크 대역에 맞게 바꿉니다. <code>\\.</code>은 진짜 점을 뜻합니다.</li>\n<li>개수에서 뺄 것: DC(질문이 \"DC 말고\"라면), 브로드캐스트(<code>.255</code>), 멀티캐스트(<code>224.x</code>, <code>239.255.255.250</code>). 기본 게이트웨이는 보통 ARP에만 나오고 IP 통신자로는 안 나옵니다.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Wireshark: 필터 <code>ip.src==172.16.0.0/12</code>(또는 조사하는 대역) → <strong>Statistics → Endpoints → IPv4</strong> → <strong>Limit to display filter</strong> → <strong>Tx Packets</strong>가 0보다 큰 IP.</p>\n<h3 id=\"pcap-device-fingerprinting\">기기 판별</h3>\n<p>기기 종류는 단서 세 가지를 합쳐서 판단합니다. 하나만 보면 틀릴 수 있습니다.</p>\n<div class=\"article-table-wrap\"><table class=\"article-table\">\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>단서</th>\n<th>얻는 방법</th>\n<th>알 수 있는 것</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>MAC 제조사(앞 3바이트, OUI)</td>\n<td><code>-e eth.src -e eth.src_resolved</code>, <strong>Statistics → Endpoints → Ethernet</strong> + <strong>Name resolution</strong></td>\n<td>제조사: Apple, Samsung, Amazon Technologies 등</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>DHCP vendor class(옵션 60)와 호스트 이름(옵션 12)</td>\n<td>위 DHCP 명령</td>\n<td><code>MSFT 5.0</code> = 윈도우, <code>android-dhcp-8.0.0</code> = 안드로이드 8.0.0, <code>dhcpcd-…</code> = 리눅스</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>DNS 질의와 HTTP User-Agent</td>\n<td>아래 명령</td>\n<td>어떤 서비스와 통신하고 어떤 소프트웨어를 쓰는지</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<pre><code>zeek-cut id.orig_h query &lt; zeek_out/dns.log | awk '$1==&quot;&lt;IP&gt;&quot;' | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn | head -30\nzeek-cut id.orig_h host user_agent &lt; zeek_out/http.log | awk -F'\\t' '$1==&quot;&lt;IP&gt;&quot;' | sort -u\nzeek-cut host software_type name unparsed_version &lt; zeek_out/software.log | sort -u\ntshark -r case.pcap -Y 'ip.src==&lt;IP&gt; &amp;&amp; (mdns || ssdp)' -V | grep -i -E &quot;server:|\\.local|_tcp&quot; | sort -u | head -40\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><code>awk -F'\\t'</code>: User-Agent에 띄어쓰기가 있어서 탭으로 칸을 나눕니다.</li>\n<li><code>software.log</code>: Zeek가 User-Agent와 배너에서 찾은 소프트웨어 요약.</li>\n<li>마지막 줄: TV, 스트리밍 기기, 프린터는 mDNS·SSDP로 스스로를 알립니다. SSDP의 <code>SERVER:</code> 헤더와 mDNS 서비스 이름(<code>_googlecast._tcp.local</code>, <code>_airplay._tcp.local</code>)이 기기 종류를 알려 줍니다.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>자주 보이는 특징:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Ubuntu</strong>: <code>connectivity-check.ubuntu.com</code>, <code>ntp.ubuntu.com</code>, <code>api.snapcraft.io</code>, <code>archive/security.ubuntu.com</code> 질의, User-Agent에 <code>Ubuntu</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>안드로이드</strong>: <code>connectivitycheck.gstatic.com</code>, <code>android.clients.google.com</code>, User-Agent <code>Dalvik/2.1.0 (Linux; U; Android 8.0.0; &lt;모델코드&gt; Build/&lt;빌드&gt;)</code>. 모델코드(예: 삼성은 <code>SM-</code>으로 시작)를 검색하면 브랜드와 모델이 나옵니다.</li>\n<li><strong>아마존 Fire 태블릿</strong>: <code>amazon</code> 도메인이 많고, User-Agent에 <code>Silk</code>나 <code>KF</code>로 시작하는 모델코드, MAC 제조사 Amazon Technologies. Echo 스피커도 아마존과 통신하므로 User-Agent와 MAC까지 확인합니다.</li>\n<li><strong>애플</strong>: <code>apple.com</code>, <code>icloud.com</code>, <code>push.apple.com</code>, User-Agent <code>iPhone</code>이나 <code>Macintosh</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Wireshark 필터: <code>dns.qry.name contains \"ubuntu\"</code>, <code>http.user_agent contains \"Android 8.0.0\"</code>, <code>dns.qry.name contains \"amazon\" || http.user_agent contains \"Silk\"</code>, <code>ip.src==&lt;IP&gt; &amp;&amp; dns.flags.response==0</code>(그 호스트의 DNS 질문만).</p>\n<h3 id=\"pcap-operating-system\">운영체제</h3>\n<p>질문이 윈도우라고 말해 주지 않으면, OS 근거를 두세 개 모읍니다.</p>\n<pre><code>tshark -r case.pcap -Y &quot;ip.src==&lt;IP&gt;&quot; -T fields -e ip.ttl | sort | uniq -c\ntshark -r case.pcap -Y &quot;ntlmssp.version.major &amp;&amp; ip.src==&lt;IP&gt;&quot; -T fields -e ntlmssp.version.major -e ntlmssp.version.minor -e ntlmssp.version.build_number | sort -u\ntshark -r case.pcap -Y &quot;smb.native_os &amp;&amp; ip.src==&lt;IP&gt;&quot; -T fields -e smb.native_os | sort -u\ntshark -r case.pcap -Y &quot;ssh.protocol&quot; -T fields -e ip.src -e ssh.protocol | sort -u\np0f -r case.pcap -o p0f.log &amp;&amp; grep &quot;&lt;IP&gt;&quot; p0f.log | grep os=\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>TTL</strong>: OS마다 시작값이 다릅니다. 윈도우는 보통 128, 리눅스·macOS·안드로이드는 64. 같은 LAN에서 캡처했다면 시작값이 그대로 보입니다.</li>\n<li><strong>NTLMSSP 버전</strong>: NTLM 인증에 윈도우 버전이 실립니다. <code>10 0 19045</code> = Windows 10 22H2, <code>6 1 7601</code> = Windows 7 SP1.</li>\n<li><strong>smb.native_os</strong>: SMB1 호스트가 알리는 OS 이름.</li>\n<li><strong>SSH 배너</strong>: <code>Ubuntu</code>, <code>Debian</code> 같은 글자가 들어 있기도 합니다.</li>\n<li><strong>User-Agent</strong>: <code>Windows NT 10.0</code> = Windows 10/11, <code>Windows NT 6.1</code> = Windows 7, <code>X11; Linux x86_64</code> = 리눅스.</li>\n<li><strong>p0f</strong>: TCP SYN 패킷으로 OS를 수동 추정하는 도구.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Wireshark: <strong>Internet Protocol → Time to Live</strong>를 열로 추가, 필터 <code>ntlmssp.version.major</code> → <strong>NTLM Secure Service Provider → Version</strong> 펼치기.</p>\n<h3 id=\"pcap-network-info\">핵심 네트워크 정보</h3>\n<p>DHCP ACK와 Kerberos가 네트워크 자체를 알려 줍니다.</p>\n<pre><code>tshark -r case.pcap -Y &quot;dhcp.option.dhcp == 5&quot; -T fields -e dhcp.option.subnet_mask -e dhcp.option.router -e dhcp.option.domain_name_server -e dhcp.option.domain_name | sort -u\ntshark -r case.pcap -Y &quot;kerberos&quot; -T fields -e kerberos.realm -e kerberos.crealm | tr '\\t' '\\n' | sort -u\ntshark -r case.pcap -Y 'dns.qry.name contains &quot;_msdcs&quot;' -T fields -e ip.src -e dns.qry.name | sort -u\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><code>dhcp.option.dhcp == 5</code>: DHCP ACK만. 서브넷 마스크 <code>255.255.255.0</code> = /24, router = 기본 게이트웨이, DNS 서버는 보통 DC, domain name = 도메인.</li>\n<li>Kerberos realm = AD 도메인.</li>\n<li><code>_ldap._tcp.dc._msdcs.&lt;도메인&gt;</code>: 윈도우 클라이언트가 DC를 찾는 질의에도 도메인이 드러납니다.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Wireshark: 필터 <code>dhcp.option.dhcp == 5</code>에서 옵션 (1) Subnet Mask, (3) Router, (6) Domain Name Server, (15) Domain Name을 읽고, 필터 <code>kerberos</code>에서 <strong>AS-REQ</strong>를 열어 <strong>req-body → realm</strong>을 봅니다. </p>\n<h3 id=\"pcap-public-ip\">공인 IP</h3>\n<p>내부에서는 사설 IP를 쓰고, 방화벽·공유기가 공인 IP로 바꿔 줍니다(NAT). 내부에서 캡처한 PCAP의 패킷 헤더에는 공인 IP가 안 보입니다. 대신 악성코드가 감염 직후 \"내 IP 확인\" 서비스에 묻는 경우가 많고, 그 <strong>응답 본문</strong>에 답이 있습니다.</p>\n<pre><code>zeek-cut -u ts id.orig_h host uri &lt; zeek_out/http.log | grep -i -E &quot;ip|myip|whatismy|ifconfig&quot;\nzeek-cut -u ts query answers &lt; zeek_out/dns.log | grep -i -E &quot;ipify|checkip|ip-api|icanhazip|myexternalip|ipinfo|wtfismyip|ifconfig&quot;\ntshark -r case.pcap -Y 'http.response &amp;&amp; http.response_for.uri contains &quot;&lt;ip-check domain&gt;&quot;' -T fields -e frame.number -e http.file_data\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><code>http.file_data</code>: HTTP 응답 본문. <code>x.x.x.x</code>나 <code>{\"ip\":\"x.x.x.x\"}</code> 같은 값이 들어 있습니다. 16진수로 나오면 끝에 <code>| xxd -r -p</code>를 붙입니다.</li>\n<li>IP 확인이 HTTPS였다면 본문이 암호화돼 읽을 수 없습니다. 다른 평문 통신(예: C2로 보낸 POST)에서 찾거나, \"PCAP으로는 확인 불가 + 이유\"를 답안에 씁니다.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Wireshark: 필터 <code>http.host contains \"ip\" || dns.qry.name contains \"ip\"</code> → 요청 우클릭 → <strong>Follow → HTTP Stream</strong> → 파란색(서버) 본문의 IP. 또는 상세 창의 <strong>Line-based text data</strong> / <strong>JavaScript Object Notation</strong>을 펼칩니다.</p>"}}, {"id": "files-and-hashes", "title": {"en": "Downloads, files and hashes", "ko": "다운로드·파일·해시"}, "html": {"en": "<div class=\"example-label\" markdown=\"0\"><strong>Scope of this section.</strong> <ul>\n<li>Listing every download, with full URL and size</li>\n<li>Extracting files from HTTP, SMB, TFTP, FTP and email without opening them</li>\n<li>Safe handling, real file type, and the MD5/SHA1/SHA256 workflow</li>\n<li>Zeek <code>uid</code> and <code>fuid</code>: following one download across logs (\"Bro UID\" questions)</li>\n<li>The key facts to record for each download, and reading macros in Word documents</li>\n</ul></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"pcap-listing-downloads\">Listing downloads</h3>\n<p>Zeek decides a file's type from its <strong>content</strong>, not its name. A Windows executable renamed <code>radiance.png</code> still shows as <code>application/x-dosexec</code>.</p>\n<pre><code>zeek-cut -u ts id.orig_h id.resp_h host uri resp_fuids resp_mime_types &lt; zeek_out/http.log | grep -i -E &quot;dosexec|msword|officedocument|zip|octet&quot;\nzeek-cut -u ts source mime_type filename seen_bytes md5 &lt; zeek_out/files.log\nzeek-cut -u ts id.orig_h id.resp_h command arg file_size &lt; zeek_out/ftp.log\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><code>resp_mime_types</code>: the real type of what the server returned. <code>dosexec</code> = Windows executable, <code>msword</code>/<code>officedocument</code> = Word document.</li>\n<li><code>resp_fuids</code>: the file ID of that response (see <a href=\"#pcap-zeek-uid\" data-scroll=\"pcap-zeek-uid\">Zeek UID and fuid</a>).</li>\n<li><code>files.log</code> <code>source</code>: which protocol carried the file (HTTP, FTP_DATA, SMB…). <code>seen_bytes</code>: how many bytes Zeek saw.</li>\n<li><code>ftp.log</code>: <code>RETR &lt;file&gt;</code> is an FTP download.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Counting downloads: count only files of concern (executables, scripts, archives, documents). The same file fetched twice has the same hash, so count it once and say so.</p>\n<p>Wireshark: <strong>File → Export Objects → HTTP / SMB / TFTP</strong> and read Hostname, Content Type, Size and Filename. <strong>Statistics → HTTP → Request Sequences</strong> shows which page led to which request.</p>\n<h3 id=\"pcap-url-and-size\">Full URL and file size</h3>\n<pre><code>zeek-cut host uri resp_mime_types &lt; zeek_out/http.log | grep dosexec | awk -F'\\t' '{print &quot;http://&quot;$1$2}' | sort -u\nzeek-cut -u ts host uri status_code response_body_len resp_mime_types &lt; zeek_out/http.log | grep dosexec\ntshark -r case.pcap -Y 'http.response &amp;&amp; http.content_type contains &quot;application&quot;' -T fields -e frame.number -e http.response_for.uri -e http.content_type -e http.content_length\nstat -c &quot;%s bytes  %n&quot; &quot;http_objs/&lt;file&gt;&quot;\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>Line 1: build answer-ready URLs: <code>http://</code> + host + uri. <code>sort -u</code> removes duplicates.</li>\n<li><code>response_body_len</code>: body size the server sent (bytes).</li>\n<li><code>http.response_for.uri</code>: the full URL a response belongs to. <code>http.content_length</code>: the size the server declared.</li>\n<li><code>stat -c \"%s bytes %n\"</code>: the real size of the carved file. If the declared and carved sizes differ, part of the transfer is missing from the capture; report both.</li>\n<li>In Wireshark, the Packet Details of a request show <strong>[Full request URI]</strong>; right-click → <strong>Copy → Value</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"pcap-extracting\">Extracting files</h3>\n<pre><code>for p in http smb tftp imf; do mkdir -p objs_$p; tshark -r case.pcap -q --export-objects $p,objs_$p; done\nls -l objs_*\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><code>--export-objects &lt;protocol&gt;,&lt;folder&gt;</code>: write every transferred file to the folder, without opening it.</li>\n<li><code>imf</code>: email messages, saved as <code>.eml</code> (read them as text to see sender, subject and attachment names).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Zeek can carve files too, and names them with the file ID:</p>\n<pre><code>mkdir zeek_x &amp;&amp; cd zeek_x\nzeek -C -r ../case.pcap local frameworks/files/extract-all-files\nls extract_files/          # extract-&lt;time&gt;-HTTP-&lt;fuid&gt;\ncd ..\n</code></pre>\n<p>FTP sends commands on port 21 and file contents on a separate data connection. tshark finds the file names; Wireshark saves the contents:</p>\n<pre><code>tshark -r case.pcap -Y 'ftp.request.command == &quot;RETR&quot;' -T fields -e frame.time_utc -e ip.src -e ip.dst -e ftp.request.arg\n</code></pre>\n<p>Wireshark: <strong>File → Export Objects → HTTP / SMB / TFTP / IMF</strong> → <strong>Save</strong> or <strong>Save All</strong>. For FTP data or raw TCP (for example netcat): filter <code>ftp-data</code> (or find the large stream in <strong>Statistics → Conversations → TCP</strong>) → <strong>Follow → TCP Stream</strong> → <strong>Show data as: Raw</strong> → <strong>Save as…</strong>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"pcap-safe-handling\">Handling samples safely</h3>\n<p>\"Safely get the hash\" means you never open or run the file and you work where a mistake cannot hurt anything.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Isolated analysis VM</strong>: Linux, no internet or LAN access, snapshot taken first. Linux has no Microsoft Office, so even a double-click cannot run a macro.</li>\n<li><strong>Extract, do not open</strong>: carve the file from the PCAP with tshark, Zeek or Export Objects.</li>\n<li><strong>Rename and remove execute permission</strong> so nothing opens or runs it by accident.</li>\n<li><strong>Check the real type by content.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Hash</strong> (MD5, SHA1, SHA256).</li>\n<li><strong>Look up the hash only</strong> on VirusTotal. Uploading the file can tip off the attacker and can expose internal data inside the document.</li>\n<li><strong>Store</strong> it in a password-protected zip and <strong>revert the snapshot</strong> when finished.</li>\n</ol>\n<pre><code>mkdir -p ~/case/doc &amp;&amp; cd ~/case/doc\ntshark -r ~/case/case.pcap -q --export-objects http,objs\nfile objs/* | grep -i -E &quot;word|composite|zip|pe32&quot;\ncp &quot;objs/&lt;name&gt;&quot; evidence.bin\nchmod a-x evidence.bin\nmd5sum evidence.bin; sha1sum evidence.bin; sha256sum evidence.bin\nzip -P infected evidence.zip evidence.bin\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><code>cp … evidence.bin</code>: a <code>.bin</code> extension stops the desktop opening it in Word. Renaming does not change the content, so the hash is the same.</li>\n<li><code>chmod a-x</code>: remove (<code>-</code>) execute (<code>x</code>) for all users (<code>a</code>).</li>\n<li><code>zip -P infected</code>: the industry convention for sharing malware samples; stops antivirus deleting it and people opening it by accident.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"pcap-file-type\">Identifying the real file type</h3>\n<pre><code>file objs_http/*\n</code></pre>\n<pre><code>objs_http/&lt;doc&gt;        : Composite Document File V2 Document ...      ← old .doc\nobjs_http/&lt;docx&gt;       : Microsoft Word 2007+                          ← .docx/.docm\nobjs_http/&lt;file&gt;       : PE32 executable (GUI) Intel 80386, for MS Windows\nobjs_http/radiance.png : PE32 executable ...                           ← exe disguised as an image\nobjs_http/&lt;payload&gt;    : data                                          ← possibly encrypted\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>A name ending <code>.png</code> with <code>PE32 executable</code> content is an executable disguised as an image.</li>\n<li><code>data</code> with a size of hundreds of KB, from an exploit kit server, is often an <strong>encrypted payload</strong>: the victim decrypts it after download, so the PCAP alone cannot give the real EXE hash. Say \"hash of the payload as transferred\" in the answer, and ask whether a host-recovered EXE exists.</li>\n<li>In <strong>Follow → HTTP Stream</strong>, a Windows executable body starts with <code>MZ</code> (and contains \"This program cannot be run in DOS mode\"); a real PNG starts with <code>.PNG</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"pcap-hashing\">Hashing</h3>\n<pre><code>for f in objs_http/*; do if file &quot;$f&quot; | grep -q PE32; then echo &quot;== $f&quot;; md5sum &quot;$f&quot;; sha1sum &quot;$f&quot;; sha256sum &quot;$f&quot;; fi; done &gt; exe_hashes.txt\nzeek-cut fuid mime_type md5 sha1 &lt; zeek_out/files.log | grep -i -E &quot;dosexec|msword|officedocument&quot;\nsha256sum objs_http/* objs_smb/* | sort | uniq -w64 -D\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>Loop: for each file, if <code>file</code> says <code>PE32</code> (<code>grep -q</code> only answers yes/no), print a separator and the three hashes; save everything to <code>exe_hashes.txt</code>.</li>\n<li>Zeek with <code>local</code> already computed MD5 and SHA1; they must match yours (SHA256 is not on by default).</li>\n<li>Last line: <code>uniq -w64 -D</code> prints every line whose first 64 characters (a SHA256) repeat, i.e. the same file seen over HTTP <strong>and</strong> SMB. This is how you prove which download was later copied to another host.</li>\n<li>Windows: <code>Get-FileHash -Algorithm MD5 &lt;file&gt;</code> (also SHA1, SHA256).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Wireshark has no hash function: save from Export Objects, then hash in a terminal. Quote file names with special characters (<code>%</code>, <code>&amp;</code>, <code>?</code>).</p>\n<h3 id=\"pcap-zeek-uid\">Zeek UID and fuid</h3>\n<p>Bro is Zeek's old name (renamed in 2018), so a \"Bro UID\" is Zeek's <code>uid</code> field. Zeek gives every <strong>connection</strong> a <code>uid</code> starting with <code>C</code> and every <strong>file</strong> a <code>fuid</code> starting with <code>F</code>. These IDs join the logs together.</p>\n<pre><code>zeek-cut -u ts uid id.orig_h id.resp_h host uri resp_fuids resp_mime_types &lt; zeek_out/http.log | grep -i dosexec\nzeek-cut fuid conn_uids mime_type &lt; zeek_out/files.log | grep dosexec    # Bro / Zeek 5 and older\nzeek-cut fuid uid mime_type &lt; zeek_out/files.log | grep dosexec          # Zeek 6+\ncd zeek_out &amp;&amp; grep -l &lt;UID&gt; *.log &amp;&amp; grep &lt;UID&gt; *.log\ngrep -l &lt;fuid&gt; *.log; ls ../zeek_x/extract_files/ | grep &lt;fuid&gt;\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><code>uid</code> in the <code>http.log</code> line of the executable = the connection that carried it (the \"Bro UID\").</li>\n<li><code>grep -l &lt;UID&gt; *.log</code>: names of every log that mentions that connection, typically <code>conn.log</code>, <code>http.log</code>, <code>files.log</code>.</li>\n<li>Following the <code>fuid</code> adds <code>files.log</code>, <code>pe.log</code> (Zeek's analysis of a Windows executable: compile time, 32/64-bit) and the carved file <code>extract-…-&lt;fuid&gt;</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<pre><code>conn.log\nfiles.log\nhttp.log\n</code></pre>\n<p>Wireshark has no Zeek UID, but numbers each TCP connection with <code>tcp.stream</code>:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Get the client port of that UID: <code>zeek-cut uid id.orig_p &lt; zeek_out/conn.log | grep &lt;UID&gt;</code>.</li>\n<li>Filter <code>ip.addr==&lt;infected IP&gt; &amp;&amp; tcp.port==&lt;id.orig_p&gt;</code>.</li>\n<li>Read <strong>Transmission Control Protocol → [Stream index: N]</strong>.</li>\n<li>Filter <code>tcp.stream eq N</code>: the same connection as the Zeek UID.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"pcap-download-key-info\">Key information for a download</h3>\n<p>Time, source IP/port, destination IP/port, protocol and bytes are all in the <code>conn.log</code> line of the download's UID; protocol details are in <code>http.log</code> with the same UID.</p>\n<pre><code>zeek-cut -u ts uid id.orig_h id.orig_p id.resp_h id.resp_p proto service duration orig_bytes resp_bytes &lt; zeek_out/conn.log | grep &lt;UID&gt;\nzeek-cut -u ts uid method host uri status_code user_agent response_body_len resp_mime_types &lt; zeek_out/http.log | grep &lt;UID&gt;\ntshark -r case.pcap -q -z &quot;conv,tcp,tcp.stream eq N&quot;\ntshark -r case.pcap -Y &quot;http.response &amp;&amp; ip.dst==&lt;infected IP&gt;&quot; -T fields -e http.server -e http.last_modified -e http.content_type | sort -u\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><code>id.orig_h/p</code> = source IP/port (the infected host); <code>id.resp_h/p</code> = destination IP/port (the server).</li>\n<li><code>proto</code> = transport (tcp); <code>service</code> = application protocol (http).</li>\n<li><code>orig_bytes</code> / <code>resp_bytes</code> = bytes sent / received; <code>resp_bytes</code> should be close to the file size.</li>\n<li><code>-z \"conv,tcp,&lt;filter&gt;\"</code>: tshark's conversation table for just that stream (start time, duration, bytes each way).</li>\n<li><code>Server</code> and <code>Last-Modified</code> headers describe the attacker's infrastructure.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>For further investigation, record per download: UTC time, full URL, domain, server IP and port, file name, size, real type, MD5/SHA1/SHA256, User-Agent, server headers, and (outside the VM) who owns the server IP. These are the IOCs used to search proxy/firewall logs and EDR for other victims. </p>\n<p>Wireshark: filter <code>tcp.stream eq N</code> → <strong>Statistics → Conversations → TCP</strong> with <strong>Limit to display filter</strong> shows Address A/B, Port A/B, Bytes A→B / B→A, Rel Start and Duration in one row.</p>\n<h3 id=\"pcap-macros\">Malicious Word documents and macros</h3>\n<p>Malicious documents usually carry a VBA macro that runs when the document opens and downloads the next stage. <strong>oletools</strong> reads macros without running anything. Use it inside the isolated VM.</p>\n<pre><code>pip install oletools\noleid evidence.bin\nolevba -a evidence.bin\nolevba --decode evidence.bin\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><code>oleid</code>: whether the document has VBA macros, encryption, external links.</li>\n<li><code>olevba -a</code>: only the analysis table, without the full code. <code>--decode</code>: also decode obfuscated strings (Base64, hex…).</li>\n</ul>\n<pre><code>|AutoExec  |AutoOpen            |Runs when the Word document is opened        |\n|Suspicious|Shell               |May run an executable file or a system command|\n|Suspicious|CreateObject        |May create an OLE object                     |\n|IOC       |http://&lt;domain&gt;/&lt;p&gt; |URL                                          |\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><code>AutoExec</code> (<code>AutoOpen</code>, <code>Document_Open</code>): runs on open.</li>\n<li><code>Shell</code>, <code>WScript.Shell</code>, <code>powershell</code>: runs Windows commands.</li>\n<li><code>URLDownloadToFile</code>, <code>XMLHTTP</code>, an <code>IOC</code> URL: downloads from the internet.</li>\n<li>Together these mean a <strong>macro downloader</strong>. Match the IOC URL to the next download in the PCAP:</li>\n</ul>\n<pre><code>zeek-cut -u ts id.orig_h host uri resp_mime_types &lt; zeek_out/http.log | awk -F'\\t' '$2==&quot;&lt;infected IP&gt;&quot;' | grep -A5 -i -E &quot;msword|officedocument&quot;\n</code></pre>\n<p><code>grep -A5</code> prints the document line and the 5 lines after it. In Wireshark, compare the User-Agent of requests after the document: macro or PowerShell downloads often have a non-browser User-Agent or none.</p>", "ko": "<div class=\"example-label\" markdown=\"0\"><strong>이 절의 범위.</strong> <ul>\n<li>모든 다운로드를 전체 URL과 크기까지 목록으로 만들기</li>\n<li>HTTP, SMB, TFTP, FTP, 이메일에서 파일을 열지 않고 꺼내기</li>\n<li>안전한 취급, 진짜 파일 종류, MD5·SHA1·SHA256 구하는 순서</li>\n<li>Zeek <code>uid</code>와 <code>fuid</code>로 다운로드 하나를 여러 로그에서 따라가기(\"Bro UID\" 문제)</li>\n<li>다운로드마다 기록할 핵심 정보, Word 문서의 매크로 읽기</li>\n</ul></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"pcap-listing-downloads\">다운로드 목록 만들기</h3>\n<p>Zeek는 파일 종류를 이름이 아니라 <strong>내용</strong>으로 정합니다. 윈도우 실행파일의 이름을 <code>radiance.png</code>로 바꿔도 <code>application/x-dosexec</code>로 잡힙니다.</p>\n<pre><code>zeek-cut -u ts id.orig_h id.resp_h host uri resp_fuids resp_mime_types &lt; zeek_out/http.log | grep -i -E &quot;dosexec|msword|officedocument|zip|octet&quot;\nzeek-cut -u ts source mime_type filename seen_bytes md5 &lt; zeek_out/files.log\nzeek-cut -u ts id.orig_h id.resp_h command arg file_size &lt; zeek_out/ftp.log\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><code>resp_mime_types</code>: 서버가 돌려준 것의 실제 종류. <code>dosexec</code> = 윈도우 실행파일, <code>msword</code>/<code>officedocument</code> = Word 문서.</li>\n<li><code>resp_fuids</code>: 그 응답의 파일 ID(<a href=\"#pcap-zeek-uid\" data-scroll=\"pcap-zeek-uid\">Zeek UID와 fuid</a> 참고).</li>\n<li><code>files.log</code>의 <code>source</code>: 파일이 어느 프로토콜로 왔는지(HTTP, FTP_DATA, SMB 등). <code>seen_bytes</code>: Zeek가 실제로 본 바이트 수.</li>\n<li><code>ftp.log</code>: <code>RETR &lt;파일&gt;</code>이 FTP 다운로드입니다.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>개수 세기: 문제가 되는 파일(실행파일, 스크립트, 압축파일, 문서)만 셉니다. 같은 파일을 두 번 받았다면 해시가 같으니 하나로 세고 그렇게 적습니다.</p>\n<p>Wireshark: <strong>File → Export Objects → HTTP / SMB / TFTP</strong>에서 Hostname, Content Type, Size, Filename을 읽습니다. <strong>Statistics → HTTP → Request Sequences</strong>는 어느 페이지가 어느 요청으로 이어졌는지 보여 줍니다.</p>\n<h3 id=\"pcap-url-and-size\">전체 URL과 파일 크기</h3>\n<pre><code>zeek-cut host uri resp_mime_types &lt; zeek_out/http.log | grep dosexec | awk -F'\\t' '{print &quot;http://&quot;$1$2}' | sort -u\nzeek-cut -u ts host uri status_code response_body_len resp_mime_types &lt; zeek_out/http.log | grep dosexec\ntshark -r case.pcap -Y 'http.response &amp;&amp; http.content_type contains &quot;application&quot;' -T fields -e frame.number -e http.response_for.uri -e http.content_type -e http.content_length\nstat -c &quot;%s bytes  %n&quot; &quot;http_objs/&lt;file&gt;&quot;\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>1줄: <code>http://</code> + host + uri로 답안에 바로 쓸 URL을 만듭니다. <code>sort -u</code>는 중복을 지웁니다.</li>\n<li><code>response_body_len</code>: 서버가 보낸 본문 크기(바이트).</li>\n<li><code>http.response_for.uri</code>: 그 응답이 어느 URL에 대한 것인지 전체 URL로. <code>http.content_length</code>: 서버가 헤더에 적은 크기.</li>\n<li><code>stat -c \"%s bytes %n\"</code>: 꺼낸 파일의 실제 크기. 서버가 적은 크기와 다르면 캡처에서 일부가 빠진 것이니 두 값을 모두 적습니다.</li>\n<li>Wireshark에서는 요청 패킷의 상세 창에 <strong>[Full request URI]</strong>가 있습니다. 우클릭 → <strong>Copy → Value</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"pcap-extracting\">파일 꺼내기</h3>\n<pre><code>for p in http smb tftp imf; do mkdir -p objs_$p; tshark -r case.pcap -q --export-objects $p,objs_$p; done\nls -l objs_*\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><code>--export-objects &lt;프로토콜&gt;,&lt;폴더&gt;</code>: 오간 파일을 열지 않고 폴더에 저장합니다.</li>\n<li><code>imf</code>: 이메일 메시지를 <code>.eml</code>로 저장합니다(텍스트로 열면 보낸 사람, 제목, 첨부파일 이름이 보입니다).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Zeek도 파일을 꺼낼 수 있고, 파일 ID를 이름에 넣어 줍니다.</p>\n<pre><code>mkdir zeek_x &amp;&amp; cd zeek_x\nzeek -C -r ../case.pcap local frameworks/files/extract-all-files\nls extract_files/          # extract-&lt;time&gt;-HTTP-&lt;fuid&gt;\ncd ..\n</code></pre>\n<p>FTP는 명령은 21번 포트로, 파일 내용은 별도의 데이터 연결로 보냅니다. 파일 이름은 tshark로 찾고, 내용은 Wireshark로 저장합니다.</p>\n<pre><code>tshark -r case.pcap -Y 'ftp.request.command == &quot;RETR&quot;' -T fields -e frame.time_utc -e ip.src -e ip.dst -e ftp.request.arg\n</code></pre>\n<p>Wireshark: <strong>File → Export Objects → HTTP / SMB / TFTP / IMF</strong> → <strong>Save</strong> 또는 <strong>Save All</strong>. FTP 데이터나 평문 TCP(예: netcat)는 필터 <code>ftp-data</code>(또는 <strong>Statistics → Conversations → TCP</strong>에서 큰 연결) → <strong>Follow → TCP Stream</strong> → <strong>Show data as: Raw</strong> → <strong>Save as…</strong>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"pcap-safe-handling\">샘플 안전하게 다루기</h3>\n<p>\"안전하게 해시 구하기\"는 파일을 한 번도 열거나 실행하지 않고, 실수해도 피해가 없는 곳에서 작업한다는 뜻입니다.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>격리된 분석 VM</strong>: 인터넷·내부망이 끊긴 리눅스 VM, 시작 전에 스냅샷. 리눅스에는 Microsoft Office가 없어 실수로 더블클릭해도 매크로가 실행되지 않습니다.</li>\n<li><strong>열지 않고 꺼내기</strong>: tshark, Zeek, Export Objects로 PCAP에서 파일만 꺼냅니다.</li>\n<li><strong>이름 바꾸고 실행 권한 빼기</strong>: 무엇도 실수로 열거나 실행하지 못하게 합니다.</li>\n<li><strong>내용으로 진짜 종류 확인.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>해시 계산</strong>(MD5, SHA1, SHA256).</li>\n<li><strong>해시만 조회</strong>: VirusTotal에 파일을 올리지 않고 해시만 검색합니다. 파일을 올리면 공격자가 탐지 사실을 알 수 있고, 문서 안의 내부 정보가 공개될 수 있습니다.</li>\n<li><strong>보관과 정리</strong>: 비밀번호 zip으로 보관하고, 끝나면 <strong>스냅샷으로 되돌립니다</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<pre><code>mkdir -p ~/case/doc &amp;&amp; cd ~/case/doc\ntshark -r ~/case/case.pcap -q --export-objects http,objs\nfile objs/* | grep -i -E &quot;word|composite|zip|pe32&quot;\ncp &quot;objs/&lt;name&gt;&quot; evidence.bin\nchmod a-x evidence.bin\nmd5sum evidence.bin; sha1sum evidence.bin; sha256sum evidence.bin\nzip -P infected evidence.zip evidence.bin\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><code>cp … evidence.bin</code>: 확장자를 <code>.bin</code>으로 바꿔 더블클릭해도 Word로 열리지 않게 합니다. 이름만 바뀌고 내용은 같아서 해시도 같습니다.</li>\n<li><code>chmod a-x</code>: 모든 사용자(<code>a</code>)에게서 실행 권한(<code>x</code>)을 뺍니다(<code>-</code>).</li>\n<li><code>zip -P infected</code>: 악성코드 샘플을 주고받을 때 쓰는 업계 관례입니다. 백신이 지우거나 누가 실수로 여는 것을 막습니다.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"pcap-file-type\">진짜 파일 종류 확인</h3>\n<pre><code>file objs_http/*\n</code></pre>\n<pre><code>objs_http/&lt;doc&gt;        : Composite Document File V2 Document ...      ← 예전 .doc\nobjs_http/&lt;docx&gt;       : Microsoft Word 2007+                          ← .docx/.docm\nobjs_http/&lt;file&gt;       : PE32 executable (GUI) Intel 80386, for MS Windows\nobjs_http/radiance.png : PE32 executable ...                           ← 이미지로 위장한 exe\nobjs_http/&lt;payload&gt;    : data                                          ← 암호화됐을 수 있음\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>이름은 <code>.png</code>인데 내용이 <code>PE32 executable</code>이면 이미지로 위장한 실행파일입니다.</li>\n<li>익스플로잇 킷 서버에서 온 수백 KB짜리 <code>data</code>는 <strong>암호화된 페이로드</strong>인 경우가 많습니다. 피해 PC가 받은 뒤 복호화하므로 PCAP만으로는 실제 EXE 해시가 나오지 않습니다. 답안에 \"전송된 페이로드의 해시\"라고 구분해 쓰고, 호스트에서 회수한 EXE가 따로 있는지 확인합니다.</li>\n<li><strong>Follow → HTTP Stream</strong>에서 윈도우 실행파일 본문은 <code>MZ</code>로 시작하고 \"This program cannot be run in DOS mode\"가 들어 있습니다. 진짜 PNG는 <code>.PNG</code>로 시작합니다.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"pcap-hashing\">해시 구하기</h3>\n<pre><code>for f in objs_http/*; do if file &quot;$f&quot; | grep -q PE32; then echo &quot;== $f&quot;; md5sum &quot;$f&quot;; sha1sum &quot;$f&quot;; sha256sum &quot;$f&quot;; fi; done &gt; exe_hashes.txt\nzeek-cut fuid mime_type md5 sha1 &lt; zeek_out/files.log | grep -i -E &quot;dosexec|msword|officedocument&quot;\nsha256sum objs_http/* objs_smb/* | sort | uniq -w64 -D\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>반복문: 파일마다 <code>file</code> 결과에 <code>PE32</code>가 있으면(<code>grep -q</code>는 있다/없다만 알려 줌) 구분선과 해시 세 개를 출력하고, 전부 <code>exe_hashes.txt</code>에 저장합니다.</li>\n<li><code>local</code>로 돌린 Zeek는 MD5·SHA1을 이미 계산해 두었습니다. 직접 구한 값과 같아야 합니다(SHA256은 기본으로 없음).</li>\n<li>마지막 줄: <code>uniq -w64 -D</code>는 앞 64글자(SHA256)가 겹치는 줄을 모두 보여 줍니다. 즉 HTTP <strong>와</strong> SMB에 같은 파일이 있는지 찾습니다. 어떤 다운로드가 나중에 다른 호스트로 복사됐는지 이렇게 증명합니다.</li>\n<li>윈도우: <code>Get-FileHash -Algorithm MD5 &lt;파일&gt;</code>(SHA1, SHA256도 같은 방식).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Wireshark에는 해시 기능이 없습니다. Export Objects로 저장한 뒤 터미널에서 해시를 구합니다. 특수문자(<code>%</code>, <code>&amp;</code>, <code>?</code>)가 있는 파일 이름은 따옴표로 감쌉니다.</p>\n<h3 id=\"pcap-zeek-uid\">Zeek UID와 fuid</h3>\n<p>Bro는 Zeek의 옛 이름(2018년 개명)이라 \"Bro UID\"는 Zeek의 <code>uid</code> 칸입니다. Zeek는 <strong>연결</strong>마다 <code>C</code>로 시작하는 <code>uid</code>를, <strong>파일</strong>마다 <code>F</code>로 시작하는 <code>fuid</code>를 붙입니다. 이 ID로 로그를 서로 연결합니다.</p>\n<pre><code>zeek-cut -u ts uid id.orig_h id.resp_h host uri resp_fuids resp_mime_types &lt; zeek_out/http.log | grep -i dosexec\nzeek-cut fuid conn_uids mime_type &lt; zeek_out/files.log | grep dosexec    # Bro / Zeek 5 이하\nzeek-cut fuid uid mime_type &lt; zeek_out/files.log | grep dosexec          # Zeek 6 이상\ncd zeek_out &amp;&amp; grep -l &lt;UID&gt; *.log &amp;&amp; grep &lt;UID&gt; *.log\ngrep -l &lt;fuid&gt; *.log; ls ../zeek_x/extract_files/ | grep &lt;fuid&gt;\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>실행파일 줄의 <code>uid</code> = 그 파일을 실어 나른 연결(\"Bro UID\").</li>\n<li><code>grep -l &lt;UID&gt; *.log</code>: 그 연결이 나오는 로그 파일 <strong>이름</strong>만 보여 줍니다. 보통 <code>conn.log</code>, <code>http.log</code>, <code>files.log</code>.</li>\n<li><code>fuid</code>로 한 단계 더 따라가면 <code>files.log</code>, <code>pe.log</code>(Zeek가 분석한 윈도우 실행파일 정보: 컴파일 시각, 32/64비트), 꺼낸 파일 <code>extract-…-&lt;fuid&gt;</code>가 연결됩니다.</li>\n</ul>\n<pre><code>conn.log\nfiles.log\nhttp.log\n</code></pre>\n<p>Wireshark에는 Zeek UID가 없지만, TCP 연결마다 <code>tcp.stream</code> 번호를 붙입니다.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>그 UID의 클라이언트 포트 확인: <code>zeek-cut uid id.orig_p &lt; zeek_out/conn.log | grep &lt;UID&gt;</code>.</li>\n<li>필터 <code>ip.addr==&lt;infected IP&gt; &amp;&amp; tcp.port==&lt;id.orig_p&gt;</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Transmission Control Protocol → [Stream index: N]</strong> 확인.</li>\n<li>필터 <code>tcp.stream eq N</code>: Zeek UID 하나와 같은 연결입니다.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"pcap-download-key-info\">다운로드 핵심 정보</h3>\n<p>시각, 출발지 IP/포트, 목적지 IP/포트, 프로토콜, 바이트는 모두 다운로드 UID의 <code>conn.log</code> 한 줄에 있고, 프로토콜 세부 정보는 같은 UID의 <code>http.log</code>에 있습니다.</p>\n<pre><code>zeek-cut -u ts uid id.orig_h id.orig_p id.resp_h id.resp_p proto service duration orig_bytes resp_bytes &lt; zeek_out/conn.log | grep &lt;UID&gt;\nzeek-cut -u ts uid method host uri status_code user_agent response_body_len resp_mime_types &lt; zeek_out/http.log | grep &lt;UID&gt;\ntshark -r case.pcap -q -z &quot;conv,tcp,tcp.stream eq N&quot;\ntshark -r case.pcap -Y &quot;http.response &amp;&amp; ip.dst==&lt;infected IP&gt;&quot; -T fields -e http.server -e http.last_modified -e http.content_type | sort -u\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><code>id.orig_h/p</code> = 출발지 IP/포트(감염 호스트), <code>id.resp_h/p</code> = 목적지 IP/포트(서버).</li>\n<li><code>proto</code> = 전송 프로토콜(tcp), <code>service</code> = 응용 프로토콜(http).</li>\n<li><code>orig_bytes</code> / <code>resp_bytes</code> = 보낸 / 받은 바이트. <code>resp_bytes</code>는 파일 크기와 비슷해야 합니다.</li>\n<li><code>-z \"conv,tcp,&lt;필터&gt;\"</code>: 그 연결만의 tshark 대화 표(시작 시각, 지속 시간, 방향별 바이트).</li>\n<li><code>Server</code>, <code>Last-Modified</code> 헤더는 공격자 인프라의 특징입니다.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>추가 조사용으로 다운로드마다 기록할 것: UTC 시각, 전체 URL, 도메인, 서버 IP·포트, 파일 이름, 크기, 진짜 종류, MD5·SHA1·SHA256, User-Agent, 서버 헤더, (VM 밖에서 조회한) 서버 IP 소유자. 이것이 프록시·방화벽 로그와 EDR에서 다른 피해자를 찾을 때 쓰는 IOC(침해 지표)입니다. </p>\n<p>Wireshark: 필터 <code>tcp.stream eq N</code> → <strong>Statistics → Conversations → TCP</strong>에서 <strong>Limit to display filter</strong>를 체크하면 Address A/B, Port A/B, Bytes A→B / B→A, Rel Start, Duration이 한 줄에 보입니다.</p>\n<h3 id=\"pcap-macros\">악성 Word 문서와 매크로</h3>\n<p>악성 문서에는 보통 문서를 열면 자동 실행돼 다음 단계를 내려받는 VBA 매크로가 들어 있습니다. <strong>oletools</strong>는 아무것도 실행하지 않고 매크로를 읽어 줍니다. 격리 VM 안에서 씁니다.</p>\n<pre><code>pip install oletools\noleid evidence.bin\nolevba -a evidence.bin\nolevba --decode evidence.bin\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><code>oleid</code>: VBA 매크로, 암호화, 외부 링크가 있는지.</li>\n<li><code>olevba -a</code>: 전체 코드 없이 분석 표만. <code>--decode</code>: 일부러 꼬아 둔 문자열(Base64, 16진수 등)도 풀어서 보여 줌.</li>\n</ul>\n<pre><code>|AutoExec  |AutoOpen            |Runs when the Word document is opened        |\n|Suspicious|Shell               |May run an executable file or a system command|\n|Suspicious|CreateObject        |May create an OLE object                     |\n|IOC       |http://&lt;domain&gt;/&lt;p&gt; |URL                                          |\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><code>AutoExec</code>(<code>AutoOpen</code>, <code>Document_Open</code>): 문서를 열면 자동 실행.</li>\n<li><code>Shell</code>, <code>WScript.Shell</code>, <code>powershell</code>: 윈도우 명령 실행.</li>\n<li><code>URLDownloadToFile</code>, <code>XMLHTTP</code>, <code>IOC</code> URL: 인터넷에서 파일 다운로드.</li>\n<li>이 조합이면 <strong>매크로 다운로더</strong>입니다. IOC URL을 PCAP의 다음 다운로드와 맞춰 봅니다.</li>\n</ul>\n<pre><code>zeek-cut -u ts id.orig_h host uri resp_mime_types &lt; zeek_out/http.log | awk -F'\\t' '$2==&quot;&lt;infected IP&gt;&quot;' | grep -A5 -i -E &quot;msword|officedocument&quot;\n</code></pre>\n<p><code>grep -A5</code>는 문서 줄과 그 뒤 5줄을 함께 보여 줍니다. Wireshark에서는 문서 다음 요청들의 User-Agent를 비교합니다. 매크로나 PowerShell이 보낸 요청은 User-Agent가 브라우저와 다르거나 없는 경우가 많습니다.</p>"}}, {"id": "malware-delivery-and-c2", "title": {"en": "Delivery, malware type and C2", "ko": "전달 방식·악성코드 종류·C2"}, "html": {"en": "<div class=\"example-label\" markdown=\"0\"><strong>Scope of this section.</strong> <ul>\n<li>Reading Snort alerts to name the malware family and type</li>\n<li>How the malware arrived: email attachment, link download or exploit kit</li>\n<li>What tool started a download (PowerShell, certutil, BITS, curl, browser…)</li>\n<li>Whether the source was internal or external, and who started the connection</li>\n<li>Post-infection traffic: C2 IP and domain, beacons, scans, exfiltration</li>\n<li>Commonly reported patterns (Emotet, IcedID, Trickbot) as hints to confirm, not answers</li>\n</ul></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"pcap-snort-alerts\">Reading Snort alerts</h3>\n<pre><code>grep &quot;&lt;infected IP&gt;&quot; snort_out/alert | grep -oE &quot;\\] ET [^\\[]*\\[\\*\\*\\] \\[Classification: [^]]*\\]&quot; | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><code>\\] ET [^\\[]*</code>: the rule name (<code>ET MALWARE …</code>).</li>\n<li><code>\\[Classification: …\\]</code>: Snort's risk category, for example \"A Network Trojan was detected\".</li>\n</ul>\n<pre><code>     20 ] ET MALWARE &lt;name&gt; CnC Checkin [**] [Classification: A Network Trojan was detected]\n      5 ] ET EXPLOIT_KIT &lt;name&gt; Landing [**] [Classification: ...]\n</code></pre>\n<p>The second word of an ET rule name is the category:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>EXPLOIT_KIT</code>: exploit kit activity. <code>CURRENT_EVENTS</code>: active campaigns (phishing, EK).</li>\n<li><code>MALWARE</code>, <code>TROJAN</code>: the malware itself. <code>POLICY</code>, <code>INFO</code>: notable but not necessarily malicious.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"pcap-malware-type\">Malware family and type</h3>\n<p>Name the family if the alert or hash lookup names one, and the type from these words:</p>\n<div class=\"article-table-wrap\"><table class=\"article-table\">\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Word in alert or hash report</th>\n<th>Type</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Ransomware</code>, <code>Ransom</code></td>\n<td>Ransomware: encrypts files and demands payment</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Banker</code>, <code>Banking</code></td>\n<td>Banking trojan: steals banking credentials</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Stealer</code></td>\n<td>Information stealer: passwords, browser data</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>RAT</code></td>\n<td>Remote access trojan</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Loader</code>, <code>Downloader</code>, <code>Dropper</code></td>\n<td>Fetches and installs other malware</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>CnC</code>, <code>Checkin</code>, <code>Beacon</code></td>\n<td>Post-infection communication with C2</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Confirm with hashes: search each SHA256 on VirusTotal (hash only) and note the <strong>Family labels</strong> and common detection names. When several files are involved, list them in download order: a downloader followed by a different family is a <strong>multi-stage infection</strong> (one malware bringing in another).</p>\n<p>Wireshark has no alert engine. Use it to check an alert: filter <code>ip.addr==x.x.x.x &amp;&amp; tcp.port==yyy</code> from the alert line, then <strong>Follow → TCP/HTTP Stream</strong>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"pcap-delivery\">Delivery method</h3>\n<p>The delivery method is what the host did <strong>just before</strong> it received the malicious file.</p>\n<div class=\"article-table-wrap\"><table class=\"article-table\">\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Delivery</th>\n<th>What it looks like in traffic</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Malicious email attachment</td>\n<td>SMTP/POP3/IMAP traffic, a message with an attachment</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Malicious link download</td>\n<td>A direct GET for a document, zip or exe, often with no referrer (user clicked a link in an email)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Exploit kit (EK)</td>\n<td>Legitimate site → redirect → landing page (HTML/JS) → Flash/script exploit → payload, all without the user downloading anything on purpose</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<pre><code>grep -oE &quot;ET [A-Z_]+&quot; snort_out/alert | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn\nls zeek_out/ | grep -E &quot;smtp|pop|imap&quot;\nzeek-cut -u ts id.orig_h id.resp_h mailfrom rcptto subject &lt; zeek_out/smtp.log\nzeek-cut -u ts id.resp_h host uri referrer resp_mime_types &lt; zeek_out/http.log | awk -F'\\t' '$6!=&quot;-&quot; &amp;&amp; $6!~/image|css|javascript/'\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><code>EXPLOIT_KIT</code> alerts point to an exploit kit; read their full names and server IPs.</li>\n<li><code>smtp.log</code> exists only if mail was sent in clear text. Webmail is HTTPS and invisible.</li>\n<li>The last line lists responses with a file type, minus images/CSS/JS, with the <code>referrer</code> (which page led there). Read it downward:<ul>\n<li><code>text/html</code> → <code>application/x-shockwave-flash</code> → <code>application/octet-stream</code>/<code>x-dosexec</code> from the same unfamiliar server = exploit kit. That server's IP is the delivery IP.</li>\n<li><code>msword</code>, <code>zip</code> or <code>x-dosexec</code> straight away with no referrer = link download. The server that gave the file is the delivery IP.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Wireshark: <strong>Statistics → HTTP → Request Sequences</strong> (referrer tree); <strong>File → Export Objects → HTTP</strong> read top to bottom; filter <code>smtp || pop || imap</code> and <strong>Export Objects → IMF</strong> for email.</p>\n<h3 id=\"pcap-download-tool\">What started the download</h3>\n<p>\"What did the host start the download with?\" asks which program or command requested the file. The HTTP User-Agent is the main clue; an unencrypted shell session can show the actual command.</p>\n<pre><code>zeek-cut -u ts id.orig_h host uri user_agent &lt; zeek_out/http.log | awk -F'\\t' '$2==&quot;&lt;infected IP&gt;&quot;'\n</code></pre>\n<div class=\"article-table-wrap\"><table class=\"article-table\">\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>User-Agent contains</th>\n<th>Tool</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>WindowsPowerShell/5.1</code></td>\n<td>PowerShell <code>Invoke-WebRequest</code> / <code>iwr</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Microsoft-CryptoAPI/10.0</code></td>\n<td><code>certutil -urlcache -f</code> (a built-in tool abused for downloads)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Microsoft BITS/7.x</code></td>\n<td><code>bitsadmin</code> or the BITS service</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>curl/x.x</code></td>\n<td>curl (built into Windows 10 and later)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Wget/x.x</code></td>\n<td>wget (mostly Linux)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Python-urllib</code>, <code>python-requests</code></td>\n<td>A Python script</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Mozilla/5.0 … Chrome/…</code> etc.</td>\n<td>A web browser (user clicked a link)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>empty (<code>-</code>)</td>\n<td>Custom tool or malware</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>User-Agents can be faked, so confirm with the commands if you can see them:</p>\n<pre><code>tshark -r case.pcap -Y 'tcp contains &quot;powershell&quot; || tcp contains &quot;certutil&quot; || tcp contains &quot;wget&quot; || tcp contains &quot;curl&quot; || tcp contains &quot;bitsadmin&quot;' -T fields -e frame.number -e frame.time_utc -e ip.src -e ip.dst -e tcp.stream\ntshark -r case.pcap -q -z follow,tcp,ascii,&lt;stream number&gt;\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>Line 1: packets whose payload contains a download tool name (<code>contains</code> is case-sensitive), with their stream numbers.</li>\n<li>Line 2: the whole conversation as text. In a reverse shell you read the attacker's typed commands and the output like a chat.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Wireshark: <strong>Edit → Find Packet</strong> → <strong>String</strong> in <strong>Packet bytes</strong> → <code>powershell</code> → <strong>Follow → TCP Stream</strong>. The two directions appear in red and blue.</p>\n<h3 id=\"pcap-direction\">Internal or external source</h3>\n<p>Two questions: is the other side of the malicious traffic internal or external, and who started the connection?</p>\n<div class=\"article-table-wrap\"><table class=\"article-table\">\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Other side</th>\n<th>Who started it</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>External</td>\n<td>Compromised host</td>\n<td>External source: the host downloaded from, or called out to, an internet server (including a reverse shell)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>External</td>\n<td>External IP</td>\n<td>External source: the internet attacked an open port on the host</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Internal</td>\n<td>Another internal host</td>\n<td>Internal source: lateral movement from an already infected machine</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Internal</td>\n<td>Compromised host</td>\n<td>Internal source: malicious file pulled from an internal share or server</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Internal (private, RFC1918) ranges are <code>10.0.0.0/8</code>, <code>172.16.0.0/12</code> (172.16–172.31) and <code>192.168.0.0/16</code>.</p>\n<pre><code>zeek-cut -u ts id.orig_h id.orig_p id.resp_h id.resp_p service conn_state history &lt; zeek_out/conn.log | grep &quot;&lt;host IP&gt;&quot; | grep &quot;&lt;malicious IP&gt;&quot;\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><code>id.orig_h</code> is the side that started the connection.</li>\n<li><code>history</code>: the connection as letters; uppercase = originator, lowercase = responder. <code>ShAD…</code> means the originator sent SYN (<code>S</code>) and the responder answered SYN-ACK (<code>h</code>).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Wireshark: filter <code>ip.addr==&lt;host IP&gt; &amp;&amp; ip.addr==&lt;malicious IP&gt; &amp;&amp; tcp.flags.syn==1 &amp;&amp; tcp.flags.ack==0</code> shows only connection-opening SYNs; their Source started the connection. <strong>Statistics → Conversations → TCP</strong> shows Address A (usually the initiator) and Port B (the service port).</p>\n<h3 id=\"pcap-post-infection\">Post-infection traffic</h3>\n<p>After the file runs, new kinds of traffic appear. Cut the traffic at the download time and look for connections that did not exist before.</p>\n<div class=\"article-table-wrap\"><table class=\"article-table\">\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>What you see</th>\n<th>Possible meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Repeated connections to one external IP at regular intervals</td>\n<td>C2 beacon</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>A long-lived TCP connection with human-typed commands</td>\n<td>Reverse shell</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Short connections to many internal IPs or ports</td>\n<td>Internal scanning</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Connections to other internal hosts on 445, 3389, 5985</td>\n<td>Lateral movement attempts</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Unusually large outbound bytes</td>\n<td>Data exfiltration</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Many long, random-looking DNS names</td>\n<td>DNS tunnelling or DGA</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<pre><code>grep -i -E &quot;cnc|checkin|beacon|c2&quot; snort_out/alert | grep -oE &quot;[0-9.]+:[0-9]+ -&gt; [0-9.]+:[0-9]+&quot; | sort | uniq -c\nzeek-cut -u ts id.orig_h id.resp_h id.resp_p service duration orig_bytes resp_bytes conn_state &lt; zeek_out/conn.log | awk -F'\\t' '$1 &gt; &quot;&lt;download time&gt;&quot; &amp;&amp; ($2==&quot;&lt;host IP&gt;&quot; || $3==&quot;&lt;host IP&gt;&quot;)'\nzeek-cut -u ts id.orig_h id.resp_h id.resp_p &lt; zeek_out/conn.log | awk -F'\\t' '$1 &gt; &quot;&lt;download time&gt;&quot; &amp;&amp; $2==&quot;&lt;host IP&gt;&quot; {print $3&quot;:&quot;$4}' | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn | head\nzeek-cut -u ts query &lt; zeek_out/dns.log | awk -F'\\t' '$1 &gt; &quot;&lt;download time&gt;&quot;' | head -40\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>Line 1: the external IPs in C2/check-in alerts.</li>\n<li>Line 2: every connection involving the host after the download; large <code>orig_bytes</code> = exfiltration candidate.</li>\n<li>Line 3: destinations the host called after the download, counted; high counts = beacon or scan.</li>\n<li>Line 4: DNS questions after the download.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"example-label\"><strong>IP-check sites are not C2.</strong> Malware often calls <code>api.ipify.org</code>, <code>checkip.amazonaws.com</code> and similar right after infection to learn its public IP. That is an infection signal (and the source of the <a href=\"#pcap-public-ip\" data-scroll=\"pcap-public-ip\">public IP</a>), but not the C2 server.</p>\n\n<p>From C2 IP to domain:</p>\n<pre><code>zeek-cut -u ts query answers &lt; zeek_out/dns.log | grep &quot;&lt;C2 IP&gt;&quot;\nzeek-cut -u ts id.resp_h host &lt; zeek_out/http.log | grep &quot;&lt;C2 IP&gt;&quot; | sort -u\nzeek-cut -u ts id.resp_h server_name validation_status &lt; zeek_out/ssl.log | grep &quot;&lt;C2 IP&gt;&quot; | sort -u\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>A DNS answer containing the IP gives the domain; for HTTP use the Host header; for HTTPS use the SNI (<code>server_name</code>). <code>validation_status</code> shows self-signed certificates.</li>\n<li>If none of these match, the malware talked to the IP directly with no domain; say so.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Wireshark:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Right-click the download response → <strong>Set/Unset Time Reference</strong>, then filter <code>ip.addr==&lt;host IP&gt; &amp;&amp; frame.number &gt; &lt;download frame&gt;</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Statistics → Conversations</strong> (Limit to display filter) for new peers; <strong>Statistics → I/O Graphs</strong> makes regular beacons and big transfers visible.</li>\n<li>Filter <code>dns.a == &lt;C2 IP&gt;</code> for the domain, or <strong>Statistics → Resolved Addresses</strong>.</li>\n<li>Filter <code>tls.handshake.type == 1</code>, add <strong>Extension: server_name → Server Name</strong> as a column; <code>tls.handshake.type == 11</code> → <strong>Certificate → issuer / subject</strong> (identical or nonsense values = self-signed).</li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"pcap-patterns\">Commonly reported patterns</h3>\n<p>These are patterns widely reported for well-known families. Use them to decide where to look, and only name a family that an alert or hash lookup confirms.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Emotet-style</strong>: Word document with macro → executable download → repeated HTTP POSTs straight to IP addresses (no domain in Host) on ports such as 8080 or 7080. Filter: <code>http.request &amp;&amp; http.host matches \"^[0-9.:]+$\"</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>IcedID (Bokbot)-style</strong>: follow-on HTTPS C2 to unfamiliar domains, often with self-signed certificates.</li>\n<li><strong>Trickbot-style</strong>: additional executables disguised as images (<code>.png</code> names), then spreading to other hosts such as the DC over SMB. See <a href=\"#pcap-dc-smb-and-snort-rules\" data-scroll=\"pcap-dc-smb-and-snort-rules\">SMB attacks on the DC and Snort rules</a>.</li>\n</ul>", "ko": "<div class=\"example-label\" markdown=\"0\"><strong>이 절의 범위.</strong> <ul>\n<li>Snort 알림으로 악성코드 패밀리와 종류 판단하기</li>\n<li>악성코드가 들어온 길: 이메일 첨부, 링크 다운로드, 익스플로잇 킷</li>\n<li>다운로드를 시작한 도구(PowerShell, certutil, BITS, curl, 브라우저 등)</li>\n<li>출처가 내부인지 외부인지, 누가 연결을 시작했는지</li>\n<li>감염 후 통신: C2 IP와 도메인, 비콘, 스캔, 유출</li>\n<li>자주 보고된 패턴(Emotet, IcedID, Trickbot)은 확인용 힌트일 뿐 답이 아님</li>\n</ul></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"pcap-snort-alerts\">Snort 알림 읽기</h3>\n<pre><code>grep &quot;&lt;infected IP&gt;&quot; snort_out/alert | grep -oE &quot;\\] ET [^\\[]*\\[\\*\\*\\] \\[Classification: [^]]*\\]&quot; | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><code>\\] ET [^\\[]*</code>: 규칙 이름(<code>ET MALWARE …</code>).</li>\n<li><code>\\[Classification: …\\]</code>: Snort가 붙인 위험 분류(예: \"A Network Trojan was detected\").</li>\n</ul>\n<pre><code>     20 ] ET MALWARE &lt;name&gt; CnC Checkin [**] [Classification: A Network Trojan was detected]\n      5 ] ET EXPLOIT_KIT &lt;name&gt; Landing [**] [Classification: ...]\n</code></pre>\n<p>ET 규칙 이름의 두 번째 단어가 분류입니다.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>EXPLOIT_KIT</code>: 익스플로잇 킷 활동. <code>CURRENT_EVENTS</code>: 요즘 유행하는 공격(피싱, EK).</li>\n<li><code>MALWARE</code>, <code>TROJAN</code>: 악성코드 자체. <code>POLICY</code>, <code>INFO</code>: 눈여겨볼 만하지만 꼭 악성은 아님.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"pcap-malware-type\">악성코드 패밀리와 종류</h3>\n<p>알림이나 해시 조회에 패밀리 이름이 있으면 그 이름을, 종류는 아래 단어로 판단합니다.</p>\n<div class=\"article-table-wrap\"><table class=\"article-table\">\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>알림·해시 결과에 있는 단어</th>\n<th>종류</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Ransomware</code>, <code>Ransom</code></td>\n<td>랜섬웨어: 파일을 암호화하고 돈을 요구</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Banker</code>, <code>Banking</code></td>\n<td>뱅킹 트로이목마: 은행 정보 탈취</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Stealer</code></td>\n<td>정보 탈취형: 비밀번호, 브라우저 정보</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>RAT</code></td>\n<td>원격 조종 트로이목마</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Loader</code>, <code>Downloader</code>, <code>Dropper</code></td>\n<td>다른 악성코드를 내려받아 설치</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>CnC</code>, <code>Checkin</code>, <code>Beacon</code></td>\n<td>감염 후 C2와의 통신</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>해시로 확인: SHA256을 하나씩 VirusTotal에 검색하고(해시만), <strong>Family labels</strong>와 공통으로 나오는 탐지 이름을 적습니다. 파일이 여러 개면 받은 순서대로 정리합니다. 다운로더 다음에 다른 패밀리가 오면 <strong>다단계 감염</strong>(한 악성코드가 다른 악성코드를 끌어옴)입니다.</p>\n<p>Wireshark에는 알림 기능이 없습니다. 알림 줄의 값으로 <code>ip.addr==x.x.x.x &amp;&amp; tcp.port==yyy</code> 필터를 넣고 <strong>Follow → TCP/HTTP Stream</strong>으로 알림이 맞는지만 확인합니다.</p>\n<h3 id=\"pcap-delivery\">전달 방식</h3>\n<p>전달 방식은 호스트가 악성 파일을 받기 <strong>바로 전에</strong> 무엇을 했는지로 판단합니다.</p>\n<div class=\"article-table-wrap\"><table class=\"article-table\">\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>전달 방식</th>\n<th>트래픽에서 보이는 모습</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>악성 이메일 첨부</td>\n<td>SMTP·POP3·IMAP 트래픽, 첨부파일이 있는 메일</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>악성 링크 다운로드</td>\n<td>문서·zip·exe를 바로 GET, referrer가 없는 경우가 많음(메일의 링크 클릭)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>익스플로잇 킷(EK)</td>\n<td>정상 사이트 → 리다이렉트 → 랜딩 페이지(HTML/JS) → Flash·스크립트 공격 → 페이로드. 사용자는 사이트만 방문</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<pre><code>grep -oE &quot;ET [A-Z_]+&quot; snort_out/alert | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn\nls zeek_out/ | grep -E &quot;smtp|pop|imap&quot;\nzeek-cut -u ts id.orig_h id.resp_h mailfrom rcptto subject &lt; zeek_out/smtp.log\nzeek-cut -u ts id.resp_h host uri referrer resp_mime_types &lt; zeek_out/http.log | awk -F'\\t' '$6!=&quot;-&quot; &amp;&amp; $6!~/image|css|javascript/'\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><code>EXPLOIT_KIT</code> 알림은 익스플로잇 킷을 가리킵니다. 전체 이름과 서버 IP를 읽습니다.</li>\n<li><code>smtp.log</code>는 메일이 평문으로 오갔을 때만 생깁니다. 웹메일은 HTTPS라 보이지 않습니다.</li>\n<li>마지막 줄: 파일 종류가 있는 응답에서 이미지·CSS·JS를 빼고, <code>referrer</code>(어느 페이지에서 넘어왔는지)와 함께 보여 줍니다. 위에서 아래로 읽습니다.<ul>\n<li>낯선 같은 서버에서 <code>text/html</code> → <code>application/x-shockwave-flash</code> → <code>application/octet-stream</code>/<code>x-dosexec</code> 순서면 익스플로잇 킷. 그 서버 IP가 전달 IP입니다.</li>\n<li>referrer 없이 바로 <code>msword</code>, <code>zip</code>, <code>x-dosexec</code>를 받았으면 링크 다운로드. 파일을 준 서버 IP가 전달 IP입니다.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Wireshark: <strong>Statistics → HTTP → Request Sequences</strong>(referrer 나무), <strong>File → Export Objects → HTTP</strong>를 위에서 아래로, 이메일은 필터 <code>smtp || pop || imap</code>과 <strong>Export Objects → IMF</strong>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"pcap-download-tool\">다운로드를 시작한 도구</h3>\n<p>\"무엇으로 다운로드를 시작했나\"는 어떤 프로그램·명령이 파일을 요청했는지입니다. HTTP User-Agent가 가장 큰 단서이고, 암호화되지 않은 쉘 세션이 있으면 실제 명령이 보입니다.</p>\n<pre><code>zeek-cut -u ts id.orig_h host uri user_agent &lt; zeek_out/http.log | awk -F'\\t' '$2==&quot;&lt;infected IP&gt;&quot;'\n</code></pre>\n<div class=\"article-table-wrap\"><table class=\"article-table\">\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>User-Agent에 있는 글자</th>\n<th>도구</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>WindowsPowerShell/5.1</code></td>\n<td>PowerShell <code>Invoke-WebRequest</code> / <code>iwr</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Microsoft-CryptoAPI/10.0</code></td>\n<td><code>certutil -urlcache -f</code>(다운로드에 악용되는 윈도우 기본 도구)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Microsoft BITS/7.x</code></td>\n<td><code>bitsadmin</code> 또는 BITS 서비스</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>curl/x.x</code></td>\n<td>curl(윈도우 10 이상에도 기본 포함)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Wget/x.x</code></td>\n<td>wget(주로 리눅스)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Python-urllib</code>, <code>python-requests</code></td>\n<td>파이썬 스크립트</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Mozilla/5.0 … Chrome/…</code> 등</td>\n<td>웹 브라우저(사용자가 링크 클릭)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>없음(<code>-</code>)</td>\n<td>직접 만든 도구나 악성코드</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>User-Agent는 바꿀 수 있으므로, 명령이 보이면 그걸로 확인합니다.</p>\n<pre><code>tshark -r case.pcap -Y 'tcp contains &quot;powershell&quot; || tcp contains &quot;certutil&quot; || tcp contains &quot;wget&quot; || tcp contains &quot;curl&quot; || tcp contains &quot;bitsadmin&quot;' -T fields -e frame.number -e frame.time_utc -e ip.src -e ip.dst -e tcp.stream\ntshark -r case.pcap -q -z follow,tcp,ascii,&lt;stream number&gt;\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>1줄: 패킷 내용에 다운로드 도구 이름이 들어간 패킷과 그 연결 번호(<code>contains</code>는 대소문자를 구분).</li>\n<li>2줄: 그 연결 전체를 글자로. 리버스 쉘이면 공격자가 친 명령과 결과가 대화처럼 보입니다.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Wireshark: <strong>Edit → Find Packet</strong> → <strong>String</strong>, <strong>Packet bytes</strong> → <code>powershell</code> → <strong>Follow → TCP Stream</strong>. 명령을 보낸 쪽과 결과를 돌려준 쪽이 빨강·파랑으로 구분돼 보입니다.</p>\n<h3 id=\"pcap-direction\">내부 출처인가 외부 출처인가</h3>\n<p>두 가지를 봅니다. 악성 통신의 상대가 내부인지 외부인지, 그리고 누가 연결을 시작했는지.</p>\n<div class=\"article-table-wrap\"><table class=\"article-table\">\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>상대</th>\n<th>연결을 시작한 쪽</th>\n<th>해석</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>외부</td>\n<td>침해 호스트</td>\n<td>외부 출처: 호스트가 인터넷 서버에서 다운로드하거나 C2에 접속(리버스 쉘 포함)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>외부</td>\n<td>외부 IP</td>\n<td>외부 출처: 인터넷에서 호스트의 열린 포트를 직접 공격</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>내부</td>\n<td>다른 내부 호스트</td>\n<td>내부 출처: 이미 감염된 다른 PC에서 내부 전파</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>내부</td>\n<td>침해 호스트</td>\n<td>내부 출처: 내부 공유 폴더·서버에서 악성 파일을 받음</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>내부(사설, RFC1918) 대역은 <code>10.0.0.0/8</code>, <code>172.16.0.0/12</code>(172.16~172.31), <code>192.168.0.0/16</code>입니다.</p>\n<pre><code>zeek-cut -u ts id.orig_h id.orig_p id.resp_h id.resp_p service conn_state history &lt; zeek_out/conn.log | grep &quot;&lt;host IP&gt;&quot; | grep &quot;&lt;malicious IP&gt;&quot;\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><code>id.orig_h</code>가 연결을 시작한 쪽입니다.</li>\n<li><code>history</code>: 연결 과정을 글자로 요약한 것. 대문자는 시작한 쪽, 소문자는 응답한 쪽. <code>ShAD…</code>는 시작한 쪽이 SYN(<code>S</code>)을 보내고 응답한 쪽이 SYN-ACK(<code>h</code>)를 돌려준 정상 연결입니다.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Wireshark: 필터 <code>ip.addr==&lt;host IP&gt; &amp;&amp; ip.addr==&lt;malicious IP&gt; &amp;&amp; tcp.flags.syn==1 &amp;&amp; tcp.flags.ack==0</code>은 연결을 여는 SYN만 보여 주고, 그 Source가 연결을 시작한 쪽입니다. <strong>Statistics → Conversations → TCP</strong>에서 Address A(보통 시작한 쪽)와 Port B(서비스 포트)를 함께 봅니다.</p>\n<h3 id=\"pcap-post-infection\">감염 후 통신</h3>\n<p>파일이 실행되면 새로운 종류의 통신이 생깁니다. 다운로드 시각 이후만 잘라서 \"전에는 없던 연결\"을 찾습니다.</p>\n<div class=\"article-table-wrap\"><table class=\"article-table\">\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>보이는 모습</th>\n<th>뜻할 수 있는 것</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>같은 외부 IP로 일정 간격 반복 연결</td>\n<td>C2 비콘</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>오래 열린 TCP 연결, 사람이 친 명령이 보임</td>\n<td>리버스 쉘</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>여러 내부 IP·포트로 짧은 연결</td>\n<td>내부 스캔</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>다른 내부 호스트로 445·3389·5985 연결</td>\n<td>내부 전파 시도</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>밖으로 나가는 바이트가 유난히 큼</td>\n<td>데이터 유출</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>길고 무작위 같은 DNS 이름이 많음</td>\n<td>DNS 터널링이나 DGA</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<pre><code>grep -i -E &quot;cnc|checkin|beacon|c2&quot; snort_out/alert | grep -oE &quot;[0-9.]+:[0-9]+ -&gt; [0-9.]+:[0-9]+&quot; | sort | uniq -c\nzeek-cut -u ts id.orig_h id.resp_h id.resp_p service duration orig_bytes resp_bytes conn_state &lt; zeek_out/conn.log | awk -F'\\t' '$1 &gt; &quot;&lt;download time&gt;&quot; &amp;&amp; ($2==&quot;&lt;host IP&gt;&quot; || $3==&quot;&lt;host IP&gt;&quot;)'\nzeek-cut -u ts id.orig_h id.resp_h id.resp_p &lt; zeek_out/conn.log | awk -F'\\t' '$1 &gt; &quot;&lt;download time&gt;&quot; &amp;&amp; $2==&quot;&lt;host IP&gt;&quot; {print $3&quot;:&quot;$4}' | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn | head\nzeek-cut -u ts query &lt; zeek_out/dns.log | awk -F'\\t' '$1 &gt; &quot;&lt;download time&gt;&quot;' | head -40\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>1줄: C2·체크인 알림에 나온 외부 IP.</li>\n<li>2줄: 다운로드 이후 그 호스트가 관련된 모든 연결. <code>orig_bytes</code>가 크면 유출 후보.</li>\n<li>3줄: 다운로드 이후 호스트가 나간 목적지별 횟수. 많으면 비콘이나 스캔.</li>\n<li>4줄: 다운로드 이후 DNS 질의.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"example-label\"><strong>IP 확인 사이트는 C2가 아닙니다.</strong> 악성코드는 감염 직후 <code>api.ipify.org</code>, <code>checkip.amazonaws.com</code> 같은 곳에 접속해 공인 IP를 알아내는 경우가 많습니다. 감염 신호는 맞고 <a href=\"#pcap-public-ip\" data-scroll=\"pcap-public-ip\">공인 IP</a>의 출처도 되지만, C2 서버는 아닙니다.</p>\n\n<p>C2 IP에서 도메인 찾기:</p>\n<pre><code>zeek-cut -u ts query answers &lt; zeek_out/dns.log | grep &quot;&lt;C2 IP&gt;&quot;\nzeek-cut -u ts id.resp_h host &lt; zeek_out/http.log | grep &quot;&lt;C2 IP&gt;&quot; | sort -u\nzeek-cut -u ts id.resp_h server_name validation_status &lt; zeek_out/ssl.log | grep &quot;&lt;C2 IP&gt;&quot; | sort -u\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>DNS 응답에 그 IP가 있으면 그 질의가 도메인입니다. HTTP는 Host 헤더, HTTPS는 SNI(<code>server_name</code>)를 봅니다. <code>validation_status</code>는 자체 서명 인증서를 보여 줍니다.</li>\n<li>아무것도 안 나오면 악성코드가 도메인 없이 IP로 바로 통신한 것이니 그렇게 씁니다.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Wireshark:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>다운로드 응답 패킷 우클릭 → <strong>Set/Unset Time Reference</strong>, 필터 <code>ip.addr==&lt;host IP&gt; &amp;&amp; frame.number &gt; &lt;download frame&gt;</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Statistics → Conversations</strong>(Limit to display filter)로 새 상대를 보고, <strong>Statistics → I/O Graphs</strong>로 일정 간격 비콘이나 큰 전송을 눈으로 확인합니다.</li>\n<li>도메인은 필터 <code>dns.a == &lt;C2 IP&gt;</code> 또는 <strong>Statistics → Resolved Addresses</strong>.</li>\n<li>필터 <code>tls.handshake.type == 1</code>에서 <strong>Extension: server_name → Server Name</strong>을 열로 추가, <code>tls.handshake.type == 11</code>에서 <strong>Certificate → issuer / subject</strong>(같거나 엉터리 값이면 자체 서명).</li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"pcap-patterns\">자주 보고된 패턴</h3>\n<p>잘 알려진 패밀리에서 널리 보고된 패턴입니다. 어디를 볼지 정하는 데만 쓰고, 패밀리 이름은 알림이나 해시 조회로 확인된 것만 씁니다.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Emotet 계열</strong>: 매크로 Word 문서 → 실행파일 다운로드 → 도메인 없이 IP로 바로(Host가 IP) 8080·7080 같은 포트에 HTTP POST 반복. 필터: <code>http.request &amp;&amp; http.host matches \"^[0-9.:]+$\"</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>IcedID(Bokbot) 계열</strong>: 이어서 낯선 도메인으로 HTTPS C2, 자체 서명 인증서가 많음.</li>\n<li><strong>Trickbot 계열</strong>: 이미지(<code>.png</code> 이름)로 위장한 실행파일을 추가로 받고, SMB로 DC 같은 다른 호스트에 퍼짐. <a href=\"#pcap-dc-smb-and-snort-rules\" data-scroll=\"pcap-dc-smb-and-snort-rules\">DC SMB 공격과 Snort 규칙</a> 참고.</li>\n</ul>"}}, {"id": "dc-smb-and-snort-rules", "title": {"en": "SMB attacks on the DC and writing Snort rules", "ko": "DC SMB 공격과 Snort 규칙 작성"}, "html": {"en": "<div class=\"example-label\" markdown=\"0\"><strong>Scope of this section.</strong> <ul>\n<li>Proving that malware spread from a client to the Domain Controller over SMB, and when</li>\n<li>Telling apart three kinds of failed attempts on TCP 445: MS17-010 (EternalBlue), logon guessing, ADMIN$ access</li>\n<li>Snort 2 rule syntax, line by line</li>\n<li>Example rules for each case, adding them to <code>local.rules</code>, and testing them against the PCAP</li>\n</ul></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"pcap-smb-lateral\">Lateral movement to the DC over SMB</h3>\n<p>The DC is infected when the infected client copies an executable to it over SMB (TCP 445, Windows file sharing) and runs it remotely. The time of that write is \"when was the DC infected\"; the files that crossed SMB are the \"files in the SMB traffic\".</p>\n<pre><code>zeek-cut -u ts id.orig_h id.resp_h action path name size &lt; zeek_out/smb_files.log\ntshark -r case.pcap -Y &quot;smb2.filename || smb.file&quot; -T fields -e frame.time_utc -e ip.src -e ip.dst -e smb2.cmd -e smb2.filename -e smb.file | sort -u | grep -i -E &quot;\\.exe|\\.dll&quot;\ntshark -r case.pcap -Y &quot;svcctl&quot; -T fields -e frame.time_utc -e ip.src -e ip.dst -e _ws.col.Info\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><code>action</code> <code>SMB::FILE_WRITE</code>: a file was written. A <code>path</code> of <code>\\\\&lt;DC&gt;\\ADMIN$</code> or <code>C$</code> is an administrative share.</li>\n<li><code>smb2.filename</code> / <code>smb.file</code>: file names in SMB2 and the older SMB1. <code>smb2.cmd</code> 5 = Create, 9 = Write.</li>\n<li><code>svcctl</code>: the remote Service Control protocol. <code>CreateServiceW</code> and <code>StartServiceW</code> mean the copied file was started as a service (PsExec-style).</li>\n</ul>\n<pre><code>2019-xx-xxTxx:xx:xx  &lt;client IP&gt;  &lt;DC_IP&gt;  SMB::FILE_WRITE  \\\\&lt;DC&gt;\\ADMIN$  &lt;random&gt;.exe  xxxxxx\n</code></pre>\n<p>Strong confirmation: after that time, the DC itself starts talking to the internet.</p>\n<pre><code>zeek-cut -u ts id.orig_h id.resp_h id.resp_p service &lt; zeek_out/conn.log | awk -F'\\t' '$2==&quot;&lt;DC_IP&gt;&quot; &amp;&amp; $3 !~ /^172\\.|^10\\.|^192\\.168\\./'\n</code></pre>\n<p>Carve and hash the SMB files with <code>--export-objects smb,objs_smb</code> and compare their SHA256 with the HTTP downloads (<code>uniq -w64 -D</code>, see <a href=\"#pcap-hashing\" data-scroll=\"pcap-hashing\">Hashing</a>). The family whose file reached the DC is the one that infected the DC.</p>\n<p>Wireshark: filter <code>smb2.filename contains \".exe\" || smb.file contains \".exe\"</code> → <code>Create Request File: &lt;name&gt;.exe</code> then <code>Write Request</code>; filter <code>svcctl</code>; <strong>File → Export Objects → SMB</strong>; for DC outbound traffic, <code>ip.src==&lt;DC_IP&gt; &amp;&amp; !(ip.dst==172.16.0.0/12 || ip.dst==10.0.0.0/8 || ip.dst==192.168.0.0/16)</code>.</p>\n<p class=\"example-label\"><strong>Export Objects → SMB.</strong> Only files captured completely are saved correctly. A file much smaller than expected was only partly captured; say so in the answer.</p>\n\n<h3 id=\"pcap-failed-445\">Failed attempts on TCP 445</h3>\n<p>SMB commands and the DC's status codes tell the three common cases apart. Decide from the PCAP, not from the list.</p>\n<div class=\"article-table-wrap\"><table class=\"article-table\">\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Candidate</th>\n<th>What the traffic shows</th>\n<th>Sign of failure</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>MS17-010 exploit (EternalBlue)</td>\n<td>Old SMB1; large/many <code>NT Trans</code> and <code>Trans2 Secondary</code> requests; <code>Trans2 SESSION_SETUP</code> (checking for the DoublePulsar backdoor)</td>\n<td>DC refuses SMB1, or is patched and returns errors</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Logon guessing (password spraying/brute force)</td>\n<td>Repeated Session Setup requests</td>\n<td><code>STATUS_LOGON_FAILURE</code> (0xc000006d)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Copy and remote execution (PsExec-style)</td>\n<td>Tree Connect to <code>\\\\DC\\ADMIN$</code> or <code>C$</code>, <code>.exe</code> write, <code>svcctl</code></td>\n<td><code>STATUS_ACCESS_DENIED</code> (0xc0000022)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<pre><code>grep &quot;&lt;DC_IP&gt;:445&quot; snort_out/alert | grep -oE &quot;\\] ET [^\\[]*&quot; | sort | uniq -c\ntshark -r case.pcap -Y &quot;ip.addr==&lt;DC_IP&gt; &amp;&amp; tcp.port==445 &amp;&amp; (smb || smb2)&quot; -T fields -e frame.number -e frame.time_utc -e ip.src -e _ws.col.Info -e smb.nt_status -e smb2.nt_status\ntshark -r case.pcap -Y &quot;ip.dst==&lt;DC_IP&gt; &amp;&amp; (smb.cmd == 0xa0 || smb.cmd == 0x33 || smb.trans2.cmd == 0x000e)&quot; -T fields -e frame.number -e frame.time_utc -e ip.src -e _ws.col.Info\ntshark -r case.pcap -Y &quot;ip.src==&lt;DC_IP&gt; &amp;&amp; (smb.nt_status != 0 || smb2.nt_status != 0)&quot; -T fields -e frame.time_utc -e ip.dst -e _ws.col.Info | sort | uniq -c\nzeek-cut -u ts id.orig_h id.resp_h path service share_type &lt; zeek_out/smb_mapping.log\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>Line 1: alert names with <code>ETERNALBLUE</code>, <code>MS17-010</code> or <code>DOUBLEPULSAR</code> point to the first candidate.</li>\n<li>Line 2: every SMB packet to/from the DC. <code>smb</code> = SMB1 (EternalBlue uses SMB1), <code>smb2</code> = SMB2/3. <code>_ws.col.Info</code> prints Wireshark's Info column. <code>nt_status</code> <code>0x00000000</code> = success; anything else is a failure code.</li>\n<li>Line 3: <code>smb.cmd == 0xa0</code> NT Trans (EternalBlue's large payload), <code>0x33</code> Trans2 Secondary (continuation), <code>smb.trans2.cmd == 0x000e</code> Trans2 SESSION_SETUP (DoublePulsar check).</li>\n<li>Line 4: every error the DC returned, counted.</li>\n<li>Line 5: shares the client tried to connect to. Also check <code>zeek_out/notice.log</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Wireshark: filter <code>ip.addr==&lt;DC_IP&gt; &amp;&amp; tcp.port==445</code> and read the Info column; <strong>Statistics → Service Response Time → SMB / SMB2</strong> for command counts; <strong>Analyze → Expert Information</strong> for error responses; <strong>Follow → TCP Stream</strong> on the suspicious packets. </p>\n<h3 id=\"pcap-snort-rules\">Snort rule syntax</h3>\n<pre><code>alert tcp $HOME_NET any -&gt; $HOME_NET 445 (msg:&quot;...&quot;; flow:to_server,established; content:&quot;|FF|SMB&quot;; offset:4; depth:4; classtype:attempted-admin; sid:1000001; rev:1;)\n</code></pre>\n<p>A rule is a <strong>header</strong> (before the brackets) and <strong>options</strong> (inside).</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>alert</code>: raise an alert when everything matches.</li>\n<li><code>tcp</code>: TCP packets only.</li>\n<li><code>$HOME_NET any</code>: source IP and port. <code>$HOME_NET</code> is the internal-network variable in <code>snort.conf</code>; <code>any</code> = any port.</li>\n<li><code>-&gt;</code>: direction (left to right).</li>\n<li><code>$HOME_NET 445</code>: destination IP and port. Use <code>&lt;DC_IP&gt; 445</code> to watch only the DC.</li>\n<li><code>msg:\"...\"</code>: the alert name.</li>\n<li><code>flow:to_server,established</code>: only packets sent to the server on an established connection (fewer false positives).</li>\n<li><code>content:\"|FF|SMB\"</code>: bytes that must be present. Inside <code>| |</code> is hex, outside is text. <code>FF 53 4D 42</code> starts an SMB1 header, <code>FE 53 4D 42</code> (<code>|FE|SMB</code>) an SMB2 header.</li>\n<li><code>offset:4; depth:4</code>: look from byte 4 (counting from 0) of the TCP payload, within 4 bytes. On port 445 the first 4 bytes are the NetBIOS session (length) header, then the SMB header starts.</li>\n<li><code>classtype</code>: alert category. <code>sid</code>: rule ID (1,000,000 and above for your own rules). <code>rev</code>: revision.</li>\n<li>Every option ends with <code>;</code>, and the whole rule is one line.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"pcap-example-rules\">Example rules</h3>\n<p>Use the rule that matches what you found in <a href=\"#pcap-failed-445\" data-scroll=\"pcap-failed-445\">Failed attempts on TCP 445</a>.</p>\n<p><strong>A. MS17-010 (EternalBlue) attempt</strong>: SMB1 NT Trans requests, or the Trans2 SESSION_SETUP used by DoublePulsar.</p>\n<pre><code>alert tcp any any -&gt; $HOME_NET 445 (msg:&quot;LOCAL SMBv1 NT Trans request - possible MS17-010 EternalBlue&quot;; flow:to_server,established; content:&quot;|FF|SMB|A0|&quot;; offset:4; depth:5; classtype:attempted-admin; sid:1000001; rev:1;)\nalert tcp any any -&gt; $HOME_NET 445 (msg:&quot;LOCAL SMBv1 Trans2 SESSION_SETUP - possible DoublePulsar check&quot;; flow:to_server,established; content:&quot;|FF|SMB|32|&quot;; offset:4; depth:5; content:&quot;|0E 00|&quot;; offset:65; depth:2; classtype:attempted-admin; sid:1000002; rev:1;)\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><code>|FF|SMB|A0|</code>: SMB1 marker + command <code>A0</code> (NT Trans). <code>|FF|SMB|32|</code>: command <code>32</code> (Trans2).</li>\n<li><code>|0E 00|</code> at <code>offset:65</code>: Trans2 subcommand <code>0x000e</code> (SESSION_SETUP), little-endian. 65 = NetBIOS 4 + SMB1 header 32 + word count 1 + the 28 bytes of parameters before the setup word. Check it in Wireshark by clicking the <code>Subcommand</code> field and reading its position in the bytes pane.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>B. Repeated logon failures</strong>: alert when the DC returns <code>STATUS_LOGON_FAILURE</code> many times in a short period.</p>\n<pre><code>alert tcp $HOME_NET 445 -&gt; any any (msg:&quot;LOCAL SMB2 repeated logon failures from server&quot;; flow:from_server,established; content:&quot;|FE|SMB&quot;; offset:4; depth:4; content:&quot;|6D 00 00 C0|&quot;; offset:12; depth:4; detection_filter:track by_dst, count 5, seconds 60; classtype:unsuccessful-user; sid:1000003; rev:1;)\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><code>flow:from_server</code>: this time watch the DC's responses.</li>\n<li><code>|6D 00 00 C0|</code> at <code>offset:12</code>: the SMB2 header's status field, <code>0xC000006D</code> written little-endian.</li>\n<li><code>detection_filter:track by_dst, count 5, seconds 60</code>: alert once the same destination (the client that is guessing) gets more than 5 in 60 seconds.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>C. Access to the ADMIN$ share</strong>: an SMB2 Tree Connect to <code>ADMIN$</code>.</p>\n<pre><code>alert tcp $HOME_NET any -&gt; $HOME_NET 445 (msg:&quot;LOCAL SMB2 Tree Connect to ADMIN$ share&quot;; flow:to_server,established; content:&quot;|FE|SMB&quot;; offset:4; depth:4; content:&quot;|03 00|&quot;; offset:16; depth:2; content:&quot;A|00|D|00|M|00|I|00|N|00|$|00|&quot;; classtype:policy-violation; sid:1000004; rev:1;)\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><code>|03 00|</code> at <code>offset:16</code>: SMB2 command 3 (Tree Connect).</li>\n<li><code>A|00|D|00|…</code>: Windows sends paths as UTF-16 (2 bytes per character), so a <code>00</code> sits between the letters.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"example-label\"><strong>Test before you submit.</strong> The offsets above come from the SMB header layouts. Always confirm them against the real packets in Wireshark and run the rule over the PCAP before handing it in.</p>\n\n<h3 id=\"pcap-testing-rules\">Testing rules</h3>\n<pre><code>sudo nano /etc/snort/rules/local.rules\ngrep -n &quot;local.rules&quot; /etc/snort/snort.conf\nsudo snort -T -c /etc/snort/snort.conf\nsudo snort -q -k none -A console -r case.pcap -c /etc/snort/snort.conf | grep LOCAL\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>Line 1: paste the rule into <code>local.rules</code> and save (nano: Ctrl+O, then Ctrl+X).</li>\n<li>Line 2: check <code>snort.conf</code> includes <code>local.rules</code>. Remove a leading <code>#</code> if it is commented out.</li>\n<li>Line 3: <code>-T</code> is test mode. It reports the line of any syntax error; <code>Snort successfully validated the configuration!</code> means the rules load.</li>\n<li>Line 4: <code>-A console</code> prints alerts to the screen; <code>grep LOCAL</code> shows only your rules.</li>\n</ul>\n<pre><code>xx/xx-xx:xx:xx.xxxxxx  [**] [1:1000001:1] LOCAL SMBv1 NT Trans request - possible MS17-010 EternalBlue [**] [Classification: Attempted Administrator Privilege Gain] [Priority: 1] {TCP} 172.xx.xx.xx:49xxx -&gt; &lt;DC_IP&gt;:445\n</code></pre>\n<p>No alert? Compare the bytes and offsets in your rule with the real packet, check <code>$HOME_NET</code> covers your network (<code>grep \"ipvar HOME_NET\" /etc/snort/snort.conf</code>), and increase <code>rev</code> each time you change a rule.</p>\n<h3 id=\"pcap-bytes-in-wireshark\">Finding rule bytes in Wireshark</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Click the attack packet you found.</li>\n<li>In Packet Details, click <code>SMB Header → SMB Command</code> (or SMB2 <code>Command</code>, <code>NT Status</code>). The bytes pane highlights those bytes.</li>\n<li>Right-click → <strong>Copy → …as Hex</strong> and paste into <code>content:\"|…|\"</code>.</li>\n<li>The <strong>NetBIOS Session Service</strong> line is the start of the TCP payload (offset 0). Count bytes from there to get <code>offset</code>.</li>\n</ol>", "ko": "<div class=\"example-label\" markdown=\"0\"><strong>이 절의 범위.</strong> <ul>\n<li>악성코드가 클라이언트에서 도메인 컨트롤러로 SMB를 통해 퍼졌다는 것과 그 시각 증명하기</li>\n<li>TCP 445 실패 시도 세 가지 구분: MS17-010(EternalBlue), 로그인 시도, ADMIN$ 접근</li>\n<li>Snort 2 규칙 문법 한 줄씩</li>\n<li>경우별 예시 규칙, <code>local.rules</code>에 넣고 PCAP으로 시험하기</li>\n</ul></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"pcap-smb-lateral\">SMB를 통한 DC 전파</h3>\n<p>DC 감염은 감염된 클라이언트가 SMB(TCP 445, 윈도우 파일 공유)로 DC에 실행파일을 복사하고 원격으로 실행한 시점입니다. 그 쓰기 시각이 \"DC가 언제 감염됐나\"의 답이고, SMB로 오간 파일이 \"SMB 트래픽의 파일\"입니다.</p>\n<pre><code>zeek-cut -u ts id.orig_h id.resp_h action path name size &lt; zeek_out/smb_files.log\ntshark -r case.pcap -Y &quot;smb2.filename || smb.file&quot; -T fields -e frame.time_utc -e ip.src -e ip.dst -e smb2.cmd -e smb2.filename -e smb.file | sort -u | grep -i -E &quot;\\.exe|\\.dll&quot;\ntshark -r case.pcap -Y &quot;svcctl&quot; -T fields -e frame.time_utc -e ip.src -e ip.dst -e _ws.col.Info\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><code>action</code>이 <code>SMB::FILE_WRITE</code>면 파일을 쓴 것입니다. <code>path</code>가 <code>\\\\&lt;DC&gt;\\ADMIN$</code>나 <code>C$</code>면 관리자 공유 폴더입니다.</li>\n<li><code>smb2.filename</code> / <code>smb.file</code>: SMB2와 예전 SMB1의 파일 이름 칸. <code>smb2.cmd</code> 5 = Create(열기·만들기), 9 = Write(쓰기).</li>\n<li><code>svcctl</code>: 원격 서비스 제어 프로토콜. <code>CreateServiceW</code>, <code>StartServiceW</code>가 보이면 복사한 파일을 서비스로 실행한 것입니다(PsExec 방식).</li>\n</ul>\n<pre><code>2019-xx-xxTxx:xx:xx  &lt;client IP&gt;  &lt;DC_IP&gt;  SMB::FILE_WRITE  \\\\&lt;DC&gt;\\ADMIN$  &lt;random&gt;.exe  xxxxxx\n</code></pre>\n<p>강한 확인: 그 시각 이후 DC가 직접 인터넷과 통신하기 시작합니다.</p>\n<pre><code>zeek-cut -u ts id.orig_h id.resp_h id.resp_p service &lt; zeek_out/conn.log | awk -F'\\t' '$2==&quot;&lt;DC_IP&gt;&quot; &amp;&amp; $3 !~ /^172\\.|^10\\.|^192\\.168\\./'\n</code></pre>\n<p>SMB 파일은 <code>--export-objects smb,objs_smb</code>로 꺼내 해시를 구하고, HTTP 다운로드의 SHA256과 비교합니다(<code>uniq -w64 -D</code>, <a href=\"#pcap-hashing\" data-scroll=\"pcap-hashing\">해시 구하기</a> 참고). DC까지 간 파일의 패밀리가 DC를 감염시킨 패밀리입니다.</p>\n<p>Wireshark: 필터 <code>smb2.filename contains \".exe\" || smb.file contains \".exe\"</code> → <code>Create Request File: &lt;이름&gt;.exe</code>와 그 뒤 <code>Write Request</code>, 필터 <code>svcctl</code>, <strong>File → Export Objects → SMB</strong>, DC의 외부 통신은 <code>ip.src==&lt;DC_IP&gt; &amp;&amp; !(ip.dst==172.16.0.0/12 || ip.dst==10.0.0.0/8 || ip.dst==192.168.0.0/16)</code>.</p>\n<p class=\"example-label\"><strong>Export Objects → SMB.</strong> 캡처에 끝까지 담긴 파일만 제대로 저장됩니다. 크기가 예상보다 훨씬 작으면 일부만 잡힌 것이니 답안에 적어 둡니다.</p>\n\n<h3 id=\"pcap-failed-445\">TCP 445 실패 시도</h3>\n<p>SMB 명령과 DC의 응답 상태 코드로 흔한 세 경우를 구분합니다. 목록이 아니라 PCAP을 보고 정합니다.</p>\n<div class=\"article-table-wrap\"><table class=\"article-table\">\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>후보</th>\n<th>트래픽에서 보이는 모습</th>\n<th>실패의 흔적</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>MS17-010 공격(EternalBlue)</td>\n<td>예전 SMB1, 크고 많은 <code>NT Trans</code>·<code>Trans2 Secondary</code> 요청, <code>Trans2 SESSION_SETUP</code>(DoublePulsar 백도어 확인)</td>\n<td>DC가 SMB1을 거부하거나, 패치돼 에러 응답</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>로그인 시도(비밀번호 대입)</td>\n<td>Session Setup 요청 반복</td>\n<td><code>STATUS_LOGON_FAILURE</code>(0xc000006d)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>복사와 원격 실행(PsExec 방식)</td>\n<td><code>\\\\DC\\ADMIN$</code>·<code>C$</code> Tree Connect, <code>.exe</code> 쓰기, <code>svcctl</code></td>\n<td><code>STATUS_ACCESS_DENIED</code>(0xc0000022)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<pre><code>grep &quot;&lt;DC_IP&gt;:445&quot; snort_out/alert | grep -oE &quot;\\] ET [^\\[]*&quot; | sort | uniq -c\ntshark -r case.pcap -Y &quot;ip.addr==&lt;DC_IP&gt; &amp;&amp; tcp.port==445 &amp;&amp; (smb || smb2)&quot; -T fields -e frame.number -e frame.time_utc -e ip.src -e _ws.col.Info -e smb.nt_status -e smb2.nt_status\ntshark -r case.pcap -Y &quot;ip.dst==&lt;DC_IP&gt; &amp;&amp; (smb.cmd == 0xa0 || smb.cmd == 0x33 || smb.trans2.cmd == 0x000e)&quot; -T fields -e frame.number -e frame.time_utc -e ip.src -e _ws.col.Info\ntshark -r case.pcap -Y &quot;ip.src==&lt;DC_IP&gt; &amp;&amp; (smb.nt_status != 0 || smb2.nt_status != 0)&quot; -T fields -e frame.time_utc -e ip.dst -e _ws.col.Info | sort | uniq -c\nzeek-cut -u ts id.orig_h id.resp_h path service share_type &lt; zeek_out/smb_mapping.log\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>1줄: 알림 이름에 <code>ETERNALBLUE</code>, <code>MS17-010</code>, <code>DOUBLEPULSAR</code>가 있으면 첫째 후보입니다.</li>\n<li>2줄: DC와 오간 SMB 패킷 전부. <code>smb</code> = SMB1(EternalBlue는 SMB1 사용), <code>smb2</code> = SMB2/3. <code>_ws.col.Info</code>는 Wireshark Info 열을 출력합니다. <code>nt_status</code>가 <code>0x00000000</code>이면 성공, 그 외는 실패 코드입니다.</li>\n<li>3줄: <code>smb.cmd == 0xa0</code> NT Trans(EternalBlue가 큰 데이터를 보낼 때), <code>0x33</code> Trans2 Secondary(이어 보내기), <code>smb.trans2.cmd == 0x000e</code> Trans2 SESSION_SETUP(DoublePulsar 확인).</li>\n<li>4줄: DC가 돌려준 에러를 종류별로 셉니다.</li>\n<li>5줄: 클라이언트가 연결하려 한 공유 폴더. <code>zeek_out/notice.log</code>도 확인합니다.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Wireshark: 필터 <code>ip.addr==&lt;DC_IP&gt; &amp;&amp; tcp.port==445</code> 후 Info 열 읽기, <strong>Statistics → Service Response Time → SMB / SMB2</strong>로 명령별 횟수, <strong>Analyze → Expert Information</strong>으로 에러 응답, 의심 패킷은 <strong>Follow → TCP Stream</strong>. </p>\n<h3 id=\"pcap-snort-rules\">Snort 규칙 문법</h3>\n<pre><code>alert tcp $HOME_NET any -&gt; $HOME_NET 445 (msg:&quot;...&quot;; flow:to_server,established; content:&quot;|FF|SMB&quot;; offset:4; depth:4; classtype:attempted-admin; sid:1000001; rev:1;)\n</code></pre>\n<p>규칙은 괄호 앞의 <strong>헤더</strong>와 괄호 안의 <strong>옵션</strong>으로 나뉩니다.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>alert</code>: 모두 맞으면 알림을 냅니다.</li>\n<li><code>tcp</code>: TCP 패킷만.</li>\n<li><code>$HOME_NET any</code>: 출발지 IP와 포트. <code>$HOME_NET</code>은 <code>snort.conf</code>의 내부망 변수, <code>any</code>는 모든 포트.</li>\n<li><code>-&gt;</code>: 방향(왼쪽에서 오른쪽).</li>\n<li><code>$HOME_NET 445</code>: 목적지 IP와 포트. DC만 보려면 <code>&lt;DC_IP&gt; 445</code>처럼 씁니다.</li>\n<li><code>msg:\"...\"</code>: 알림 이름.</li>\n<li><code>flow:to_server,established</code>: 연결이 맺어진 뒤 서버로 가는 패킷만(오탐 줄임).</li>\n<li><code>content:\"|FF|SMB\"</code>: 있어야 하는 바이트. <code>| |</code> 안은 16진수, 밖은 글자. <code>FF 53 4D 42</code>는 SMB1, <code>FE 53 4D 42</code>(<code>|FE|SMB</code>)는 SMB2 헤더의 시작입니다.</li>\n<li><code>offset:4; depth:4</code>: TCP 데이터의 4번째 바이트(0부터 셈)부터 4바이트 안에서만 찾습니다. 445번 포트는 앞 4바이트가 NetBIOS 세션(길이) 헤더이고 그 뒤에 SMB 헤더가 옵니다.</li>\n<li><code>classtype</code>: 알림 분류. <code>sid</code>: 규칙 번호(직접 쓰는 규칙은 1000000 이상). <code>rev</code>: 수정 횟수.</li>\n<li>옵션은 <code>;</code>로 끝내고, 규칙 전체는 한 줄로 씁니다.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"pcap-example-rules\">예시 규칙</h3>\n<p><a href=\"#pcap-failed-445\" data-scroll=\"pcap-failed-445\">TCP 445 실패 시도</a>에서 확인한 것에 맞는 규칙을 씁니다.</p>\n<p><strong>A. MS17-010(EternalBlue) 시도</strong>: SMB1 NT Trans 요청, 또는 DoublePulsar가 쓰는 Trans2 SESSION_SETUP.</p>\n<pre><code>alert tcp any any -&gt; $HOME_NET 445 (msg:&quot;LOCAL SMBv1 NT Trans request - possible MS17-010 EternalBlue&quot;; flow:to_server,established; content:&quot;|FF|SMB|A0|&quot;; offset:4; depth:5; classtype:attempted-admin; sid:1000001; rev:1;)\nalert tcp any any -&gt; $HOME_NET 445 (msg:&quot;LOCAL SMBv1 Trans2 SESSION_SETUP - possible DoublePulsar check&quot;; flow:to_server,established; content:&quot;|FF|SMB|32|&quot;; offset:4; depth:5; content:&quot;|0E 00|&quot;; offset:65; depth:2; classtype:attempted-admin; sid:1000002; rev:1;)\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><code>|FF|SMB|A0|</code>: SMB1 표시 + 명령 <code>A0</code>(NT Trans). <code>|FF|SMB|32|</code>: 명령 <code>32</code>(Trans2).</li>\n<li><code>offset:65</code>의 <code>|0E 00|</code>: Trans2 하위 명령 <code>0x000e</code>(SESSION_SETUP)를 리틀 엔디안으로. 65 = NetBIOS 4 + SMB1 헤더 32 + 파라미터 개수 1 + setup 앞 파라미터 28바이트. Wireshark에서 <code>Subcommand</code> 필드를 클릭하면 바이트 창에서 위치를 직접 확인할 수 있습니다.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>B. 로그인 실패 반복</strong>: DC가 짧은 시간에 <code>STATUS_LOGON_FAILURE</code>를 여러 번 돌려주면 알림.</p>\n<pre><code>alert tcp $HOME_NET 445 -&gt; any any (msg:&quot;LOCAL SMB2 repeated logon failures from server&quot;; flow:from_server,established; content:&quot;|FE|SMB&quot;; offset:4; depth:4; content:&quot;|6D 00 00 C0|&quot;; offset:12; depth:4; detection_filter:track by_dst, count 5, seconds 60; classtype:unsuccessful-user; sid:1000003; rev:1;)\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><code>flow:from_server</code>: 이번에는 DC의 응답을 봅니다.</li>\n<li><code>offset:12</code>의 <code>|6D 00 00 C0|</code>: SMB2 헤더의 상태 코드 칸. <code>0xC000006D</code>를 리틀 엔디안(거꾸로)으로 쓴 것입니다.</li>\n<li><code>detection_filter:track by_dst, count 5, seconds 60</code>: 같은 목적지(대입을 시도한 클라이언트)가 60초 안에 5번을 넘으면 알림.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>C. ADMIN$ 공유 접근</strong>: SMB2 Tree Connect로 <code>ADMIN$</code>에 연결하려는 요청.</p>\n<pre><code>alert tcp $HOME_NET any -&gt; $HOME_NET 445 (msg:&quot;LOCAL SMB2 Tree Connect to ADMIN$ share&quot;; flow:to_server,established; content:&quot;|FE|SMB&quot;; offset:4; depth:4; content:&quot;|03 00|&quot;; offset:16; depth:2; content:&quot;A|00|D|00|M|00|I|00|N|00|$|00|&quot;; classtype:policy-violation; sid:1000004; rev:1;)\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><code>offset:16</code>의 <code>|03 00|</code>: SMB2 명령 3번(Tree Connect).</li>\n<li><code>A|00|D|00|…</code>: 윈도우는 경로를 UTF-16(글자마다 2바이트)으로 보내서 글자 사이에 <code>00</code>이 끼어 있습니다.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"example-label\"><strong>제출 전에 꼭 시험하세요.</strong> 위 offset 값은 SMB 헤더 구조를 보고 계산한 것입니다. 반드시 Wireshark에서 실제 패킷과 맞춰 보고, PCAP에 규칙을 돌려 알림이 뜨는지 확인한 뒤 제출합니다.</p>\n\n<h3 id=\"pcap-testing-rules\">규칙 넣고 시험하기</h3>\n<pre><code>sudo nano /etc/snort/rules/local.rules\ngrep -n &quot;local.rules&quot; /etc/snort/snort.conf\nsudo snort -T -c /etc/snort/snort.conf\nsudo snort -q -k none -A console -r case.pcap -c /etc/snort/snort.conf | grep LOCAL\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>1줄: <code>local.rules</code>에 규칙을 붙여 넣고 저장(nano: Ctrl+O 저장, Ctrl+X 종료).</li>\n<li>2줄: <code>snort.conf</code>가 <code>local.rules</code>를 불러오는지 확인. 줄 앞에 <code>#</code>이 있으면 지워서 켭니다.</li>\n<li>3줄: <code>-T</code>는 시험 모드. 문법이 틀리면 몇 번째 줄인지 알려 줍니다. <code>Snort successfully validated the configuration!</code>이 나오면 성공.</li>\n<li>4줄: <code>-A console</code>은 알림을 화면에 바로 출력합니다. <code>grep LOCAL</code>로 직접 만든 규칙의 알림만 봅니다.</li>\n</ul>\n<pre><code>xx/xx-xx:xx:xx.xxxxxx  [**] [1:1000001:1] LOCAL SMBv1 NT Trans request - possible MS17-010 EternalBlue [**] [Classification: Attempted Administrator Privilege Gain] [Priority: 1] {TCP} 172.xx.xx.xx:49xxx -&gt; &lt;DC_IP&gt;:445\n</code></pre>\n<p>알림이 안 뜨면: 규칙의 바이트와 offset을 실제 패킷과 비교하고, <code>$HOME_NET</code>에 조사하는 대역이 들어 있는지 확인합니다(<code>grep \"ipvar HOME_NET\" /etc/snort/snort.conf</code>). 규칙을 고칠 때마다 <code>rev</code>를 1씩 올립니다.</p>\n<h3 id=\"pcap-bytes-in-wireshark\">Wireshark에서 규칙용 바이트 찾기</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>찾은 공격 패킷을 클릭합니다.</li>\n<li>상세 창에서 <code>SMB Header → SMB Command</code>(또는 SMB2 <code>Command</code>, <code>NT Status</code>)를 클릭하면 아래 바이트 창에 그 바이트가 강조됩니다.</li>\n<li>우클릭 → <strong>Copy → …as Hex</strong>로 복사해 <code>content:\"|…|\"</code>에 넣습니다.</li>\n<li><strong>NetBIOS Session Service</strong> 줄이 TCP 데이터의 시작(offset 0)입니다. 거기서부터 몇 바이트 뒤인지 세면 <code>offset</code>입니다.</li>\n</ol>"}}]};
